[[:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189671] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189672] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189673] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189674] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189675] {:db/id 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" "question-39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192964 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189670 :answer-field/correct "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer-field/choices ["answer-293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" "answer-7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" "answer-ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" "answer-c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/id #uuid "293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/id #uuid "7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/id #uuid "ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/id #uuid "c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/id #uuid "0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/id #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138506" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" "answer-ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" "answer-2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" "answer-756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/id #uuid "af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/id #uuid "ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/id #uuid "2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/id #uuid "18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/id #uuid "756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/id #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138510" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" "answer-2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" "answer-26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" "answer-a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" "answer-20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/id #uuid "b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/id #uuid "2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/id #uuid "26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/id #uuid "a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/id #uuid "20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" "question-acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/id #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138515" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/id #uuid "105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer-field/choices ["answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" "answer-9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" "answer-e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" "answer-097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" "answer-cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/id #uuid "482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/id #uuid "9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/id #uuid "e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/id #uuid "097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/id #uuid "cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/id #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138532" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" "answer-c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" "answer-cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" "answer-d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" "answer-2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/id #uuid "f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/id #uuid "c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" :answer/id #uuid "cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" :answer/id #uuid "d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45" :answer/id #uuid "2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6ead580b-4ede-5c00-946f-c5a855bc1388" :activity/id #uuid "6ead580b-4ede-5c00-946f-c5a855bc1388" :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048721 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" "step-b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" "step-cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" "step-ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" "step-e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e"] :activity/first-step "step-a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" :step/id #uuid "a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" :step/content 17592186192964 :step/next "step-b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" :step/id #uuid "b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" :step/content "question-43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :step/next "step-cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" :step/id #uuid "cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" :step/content "question-39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :step/next "step-ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" :step/id #uuid "ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" :step/content "question-a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :step/next "step-e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e" :step/id #uuid "e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e" :step/content "question-acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083242 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e698b54-7918-5ca3-b22c-625ba5353447"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rationals Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e698b54-7918-5ca3-b22c-625ba5353447" :question/id #uuid "7e698b54-7918-5ca3-b22c-625ba5353447" :question/math-academy-id "q-14875" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{{T}^{2}-7T+12}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{(T-3)(T-4)}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(T-3,\\,T-4,\\,(T-3)(T-4))=(T-3)(T-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $T=3$ and $T=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{(T-3)(T-4)} & =0 \\\\ (T-3)(T-4)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{T-3}+\\frac{5}{T-4}-\\frac{5}{(T-3)(T-4)}) & =(T-3)(T-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{2(T-3)(T-4)}{T-3}+\\frac{5(T-3)(T-4)}{T-4}-\\frac{5(T-3)(T-4)}{(T-3)(T-4)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(T-3)}(T-4)}{\\cancel{T-3}}+\\frac{5(T-3)\\cdot \\cancel{(T-4)}}{\\cancel{T-4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{(T-3)(T-4)}}{\\cancel{(T-3)(T-4)}} & =0 \\\\ 2(T-4)+5(T-3)-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSolving this equation for $T,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(T-4)+5(T-3)-5 & =0 \\\\ 2T-8+5T-15-5 & =0 \\\\ 7T & =28 \\\\ T & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHowever, since $T=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13145ccd-33c1-5ecc-b786-0dbed884599f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13145ccd-33c1-5ecc-b786-0dbed884599f" :answer-field/id #uuid "13145ccd-33c1-5ecc-b786-0dbed884599f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ea0cad2-54d5-5918-a350-44f9f4adbe06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d732264f-ff9e-5f6c-81dc-85556b6bedbf" "answer-9e3b37ce-fd4e-5058-8a5a-eb6f6062fbb4" "answer-3a3d85f8-1d27-599f-b931-f3c725d977e2" "answer-a3909214-29f3-5e99-b133-ddc072b39827" "answer-9ea0cad2-54d5-5918-a350-44f9f4adbe06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d732264f-ff9e-5f6c-81dc-85556b6bedbf" :answer/id #uuid "d732264f-ff9e-5f6c-81dc-85556b6bedbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "T=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e3b37ce-fd4e-5058-8a5a-eb6f6062fbb4" :answer/id #uuid "9e3b37ce-fd4e-5058-8a5a-eb6f6062fbb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "T=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a3d85f8-1d27-599f-b931-f3c725d977e2" :answer/id #uuid "3a3d85f8-1d27-599f-b931-f3c725d977e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "T=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3909214-29f3-5e99-b133-ddc072b39827" :answer/id #uuid "a3909214-29f3-5e99-b133-ddc072b39827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "T=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ea0cad2-54d5-5918-a350-44f9f4adbe06" :answer/id #uuid "9ea0cad2-54d5-5918-a350-44f9f4adbe06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083412 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064236 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c9d6ed6b-fbf8-57c0-b691-2915931ac208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9d6ed6b-fbf8-57c0-b691-2915931ac208" :question/id #uuid "c9d6ed6b-fbf8-57c0-b691-2915931ac208" :question/math-academy-id "q-114886" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & 2 & 4 & 10 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & 5 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}={x}^{2}+2x+5+\\frac{9}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+x-1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x+5+\\frac{9}{x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+5x\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})+({1}^{2}+{0}^{2})+5(1-0)+9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0-2|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1+5+9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+6-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2077670f-b2d2-5635-90e0-1a5bc69c2121"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2077670f-b2d2-5635-90e0-1a5bc69c2121" :answer-field/id #uuid "2077670f-b2d2-5635-90e0-1a5bc69c2121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de9a1ad2-05e9-52eb-a0d5-b42eb5191ab2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-528bf807-f7c8-5d89-ae8b-52082e42ff70" "answer-de9a1ad2-05e9-52eb-a0d5-b42eb5191ab2" "answer-ad15a888-28a0-51eb-b31c-7a799c730314" "answer-96be4dfe-e4e2-5571-943a-fa6959f16012" "answer-4f5b864f-ab9e-5526-a0c2-c02feff7679e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-528bf807-f7c8-5d89-ae8b-52082e42ff70" :answer/id #uuid "528bf807-f7c8-5d89-ae8b-52082e42ff70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de9a1ad2-05e9-52eb-a0d5-b42eb5191ab2" :answer/id #uuid "de9a1ad2-05e9-52eb-a0d5-b42eb5191ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad15a888-28a0-51eb-b31c-7a799c730314" :answer/id #uuid "ad15a888-28a0-51eb-b31c-7a799c730314" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96be4dfe-e4e2-5571-943a-fa6959f16012" :answer/id #uuid "96be4dfe-e4e2-5571-943a-fa6959f16012" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f5b864f-ab9e-5526-a0c2-c02feff7679e" :answer/id #uuid "4f5b864f-ab9e-5526-a0c2-c02feff7679e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064244 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e3082ec-c2a4-52d5-af74-aff0c23f65d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e3082ec-c2a4-52d5-af74-aff0c23f65d8" :question/id #uuid "9e3082ec-c2a4-52d5-af74-aff0c23f65d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-358839" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{23}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $5$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5(x+1/5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{(x+1/5)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{5}$ | $8$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{8}{5}$ |\n|  | $8$ | $-\\frac{23}{5}$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-3}{(x+1/5)} & =8-\\frac{(23/5)}{(x+1/5)} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{23}{5x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-3}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8-\\frac{23}{5x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x-23{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[8x-\\frac{23}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[8(1-0)-\\frac{23}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[8-\\frac{23\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{23}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-123c7483-9bc5-51d0-9fff-02d7158c75ec" "answer-field-2a416796-2530-56e2-9658-fd2726d65c72"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-123c7483-9bc5-51d0-9fff-02d7158c75ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "123c7483-9bc5-51d0-9fff-02d7158c75ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-287ac481-01e4-501d-92a0-d611e24907eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-287ac481-01e4-501d-92a0-d611e24907eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-287ac481-01e4-501d-92a0-d611e24907eb" :answer/id #uuid "287ac481-01e4-501d-92a0-d611e24907eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a416796-2530-56e2-9658-fd2726d65c72" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a416796-2530-56e2-9658-fd2726d65c72" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b15dd44d-79f8-5979-b1d9-ef4007b4896c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b15dd44d-79f8-5979-b1d9-ef4007b4896c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b15dd44d-79f8-5979-b1d9-ef4007b4896c" :answer/id #uuid "b15dd44d-79f8-5979-b1d9-ef4007b4896c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-abd7f8c6-92bf-58ba-8f77-35b892e03ec2" :activity/id #uuid "abd7f8c6-92bf-58ba-8f77-35b892e03ec2" :activity/title "Integrating Functions Using Polynomial Division" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050299 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-6b313935-bcb5-5009-bac0-2daf40e6de4d" "step-8c494919-3b9e-595f-a17c-ad5906d7c222" "step-fa2397e7-9076-5f64-a56a-0ac0b05bc47d" "step-493b3de4-1cb9-5807-ae21-3adf761e5283" "step-c20a359b-fc4b-5f35-97e3-15ab5d7d2b17"] :activity/first-step "step-6b313935-bcb5-5009-bac0-2daf40e6de4d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6b313935-bcb5-5009-bac0-2daf40e6de4d" :step/id #uuid "6b313935-bcb5-5009-bac0-2daf40e6de4d" :step/content 17592186064865 :step/next "step-8c494919-3b9e-595f-a17c-ad5906d7c222" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8c494919-3b9e-595f-a17c-ad5906d7c222" :step/id #uuid "8c494919-3b9e-595f-a17c-ad5906d7c222" :step/content "question-c9d6ed6b-fbf8-57c0-b691-2915931ac208" :step/next "step-fa2397e7-9076-5f64-a56a-0ac0b05bc47d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fa2397e7-9076-5f64-a56a-0ac0b05bc47d" :step/id #uuid "fa2397e7-9076-5f64-a56a-0ac0b05bc47d" :step/conten