ate]} {:db/id "step-7b8cd9ab-93c4-58bf-896c-f561774db70b" :step/id #uuid "7b8cd9ab-93c4-58bf-896c-f561774db70b" :step/content 17592186308411 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" "question-18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" :question/id #uuid "51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" :question/math-academy-id "q-127236" :question/problem "Find the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $f(4)=-5$ and ${f}^{′}(4)=-7,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5-7(x-4) \\\\  & =-5-7x+28 \\\\  & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd8c605a-0b0c-5f15-b9c1-1d00ad746eb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd8c605a-0b0c-5f15-b9c1-1d00ad746eb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8c605a-0b0c-5f15-b9c1-1d00ad746eb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff" :answer/id #uuid "fce48f9d-e707-5ce1-a1fd-62602568edff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23-7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" :question/id #uuid "18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127237" :question/problem "Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $f(4.1).$\n\n$f(4.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(4.1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=4.1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1) & ≈L(4.1) \\\\  & =-7(4.1)+23 \\\\  & =-5.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2e837d9-716d-555b-80cd-9638260052b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2e837d9-716d-555b-80cd-9638260052b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e837d9-716d-555b-80cd-9638260052b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab" :answer/id #uuid "7498ef89-34e7-514e-8404-1f831fa5ecab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" "question-b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" :question/id #uuid "f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" :question/math-academy-id "q-127238" :question/problem "Write the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=3,$ we plug $a=3$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $f(3)=6,$ ${f}^{′}(3)=-5,$ and ${f}^{″}(3)=-6$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(-6)(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6abdcfbb-d03d-54ab-93d9-988a3323ce36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6abdcfbb-d03d-54ab-93d9-988a3323ce36" :answer-field/id #uuid "6abdcfbb-d03d-54ab-93d9-988a3323ce36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348" :answer/id #uuid "5214b1a9-c534-56c3-8ec8-70ac71717348" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8" :question/id #uuid "b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127239" :question/problem "Use the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 3 to estimate the value of $f(3.2).$\n\n$f(3.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our second-degree Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈{P}_{2}(3.2) \\\\  & =6-5(3.2-3)-3(3.2-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(0.2)-3(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =4.88.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ff2781d-f48f-5ca8-a7f3-24940603f906"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ff2781d-f48f-5ca8-a7f3-24940603f906" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff2781d-f48f-5ca8-a7f3-24940603f906" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1" :answer/id #uuid "cea6019d-616b-5e3e-bc27-cf4bb2985ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184187 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967" :question/id #uuid "f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967" :question/math-academy-id "q-127240" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe value of ${f}^{″}(4)=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're given an expansion about the point $x=4,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(4)=-10.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d6d2873-9a05-5246-b704-4e9d162b2b64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d6d2873-9a05-5246-b704-4e9d162b2b64" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6d2873-9a05-5246-b704-4e9d162b2b64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3" :answer/id #uuid "789eba54-30db-542e-b5bb-860f1c1b85f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100938 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" "question-adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" :question/id #uuid "c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127241" :question/problem "Write the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4.$\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2!}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{3!}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=-5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4)=-7 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4)=-10 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(4)=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor Polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-5-7(x-4)+\\frac{(-10)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(-13)}{6}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d214404-722b-5c57-9c39-86b4d12c6c9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d214404-722b-5c57-9c39-86b4d12c6c9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d214404-722b-5c57-9c39-86b4d12c6c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a" :answer/id #uuid "ed2c79e8-e15f-5688-8db4-87b555f6704a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^2-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b" :question/id #uuid "adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127242" :question/problem "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.9).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$f(3.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation is\n\n$$\n{P}_{3}(x)=-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.9)$ by evaluating the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9) & ≈{P}_{3}(3.9) \\\\  & =-5-7(3.9-4)-5(3.9-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(3.9-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(-0.1)-5(-0.1)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(-0.1)^{3} \\\\  & ≈-4.348.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f443cc95-d040-59a2-a1e7-0a2ba700ead3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f443cc95-d040-59a2-a1e7-0a2ba700ead3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f443cc95-d040-59a2-a1e7-0a2ba700ead3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e" :answer/id #uuid "3112a79b-eeb9-5709-b9a4-cb3eb1b6fa7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.348" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296" :question/id #uuid "a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296" :question/math-academy-id "q-127243" :question/problem "According to the Lagrange error bound, the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=4$ approximates $f(3.9)$ with an error that is less than{{field-1}}\n(Do not round your answer, write it as a fraction)." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-4{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=4$ and $x=3.91.$ Plugging $x=3.9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3.9-4{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}(0.1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to find the maximum of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval $[3.9,4].$\n\nSince $f$ and all its derivatives are decreasing on the interval $[2,4],$ and since ${f}^{(4)}(x)$ is negative in this interval, we know that $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ is an increasing function on the interval $[3.9,4]$ and its value is greatest at $x=4.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(4)|=|-6|=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{6}{24}(0.1)^{4}=\\frac{1}{40\\,000}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-568b31fb-dca1-54e0-b91d-7f6b4c28406c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-568b31fb-dca1-54e0-b91d-7f6b4c28406c" :answer-field/id #uuid "568b31fb-dca1-54e0-b91d-7f6b4c28406c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef" :answer/id #uuid "7b0a32aa-2dda-5dbe-95a9-d4a8abaca8ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40000}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-8fc7ff53-1fcb-5909-be7f-1625560888f4" :multistep/id #uuid "8fc7ff53-1fcb-5909-be7f-1625560888f4" :multistep/context "Let $f(x)$ be a continuously differentiable function defined for all real $x.$ Values for $f$ and some of its derivatives at particular points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n| **$x$** | **$f(x)$** | **${f}^{′}(x)$** | **${f}^{″}(x)$** | **${f}^{‴}(x)$** | **${f}^{(4)}(x)$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $9$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $4$ | $-5$ | $-7$ | $∗$ | $-13$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn the interval $x\\in [2,4],$ the function $f$ and its derivatives are all decreasing." :multistep/steps ["step-9cc5d539-75d9-59df-a31a-59a1c83afa0f" "step-d637ca93-9746-5365-8d1c-8f3d9d39791f" "step-7e50a860-e18c-5d32-aa46-f0428b0e5f3b" "step-57000baf-4e57-5c3f-9071-c200d2ee5ca5" "step-2b687f33-c2d5-5f9c-a584-2f9511726de7" "step-0580bb03-6661-59c7-abd2-59ce43dffb45" "step-8ca132fc-de6e-59cd-aec9-a613eb401d7d" "step-03306394-e329-5b33-84ba-17768bef84ce"] :multistep/first-step "step-9cc5d539-75d9-59df-a31a-59a1c83afa0f" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-9cc5d539-75d9-59df-a31a-59a1c83afa0f" :step/id #uuid "9cc5d539-75d9-59df-a31a-59a1c83afa0f" :step/content "question-51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" :step/next "step-d637ca93-9746-5365-8d1c-8f3d9d39791f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d637ca93-9746-5365-8d1c-8f3d9d39791f" :step/id #uuid "d637ca93-9746-5365-8d1c-8f3d9d39791f" :step/content "question-18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" :step/next "step-7e50a860-e18c-5d32-aa46-f0428b0e5f3b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7e50a860-e18c-5d32-aa46-f0428b0e5f3b" :step/id #uuid "7e50a860-e18c-5d32-aa46-f0428b0e5f3b" :step/content "question-f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" :step/next "step-57000baf-4e57-5c3f-9071-c200d2ee5ca5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-57000baf-4e57-5c3f-9071-c200d2ee5ca5" :step/id #uuid "57000baf-4e57-5c3f-9071-c200d2ee5ca5" :step/content "question-b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8" :step/next "step-2b687f33-c2d5-5f9c-a584-2f9511726de7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2b687f33-c2d5-5f9c-a584-2f9511726de7" :step/id #uuid "2b687f33-c2d5-5f9c-a584-2f9511726de7" :step/content "question-f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967" :step/next "step-0580bb03-6661-59c7-abd2-59ce43dffb45" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0580bb03-6661-59c7-abd2-59ce43dffb45" :step/id #uuid "0580bb03-6661-59c7-abd2-59ce43dffb45" :step/content "question-c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" :step/next "step-8ca132fc-de6e-59cd-aec9-a613eb401d7d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8ca132fc-de6e-59cd-aec9-a613eb401d7d" :step/id #uuid "8ca132fc-de6e-59cd-aec9-a613eb401d7d" :step/content "question-adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b" :step/next "step-03306394-e329-5b33-84ba-17768bef84ce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-03306394-e329-5b33-84ba-17768bef84ce" :step/id #uuid "03306394-e329-5b33-84ba-17768bef84ce" :step/content "question-a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-79110b9e-fd73-5efb-8ce0-acef8691262c" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "79110b9e-fd73-5efb-8ce0-acef8691262c" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-8fc7ff53-1fcb-5909-be7f-1625560888f4" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186049375 17592186049019 17592186050037 17592186050866 17592186049507] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ffa18ed0-9448-54b7-a3a7-aa31bdbd85d1" :activity/id #uuid "ffa18ed0-9448-54b7-a3a7-aa31bdbd85d1" :activity/title "Calculating First, Second, and Third-Degree Taylor Polynomials, and the Lagrange Error Bound" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-bcb39987-b08b-54c5-8afc-f4b4bebdd9f6"] :activity/first-step "step-bcb39987-b08b-54c5-8afc-f4b4bebdd9f6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bcb39987-b08b-54c5-8afc-f4b4bebdd9f6" :step/id #uuid "bcb39987-b08b-54c5-8afc-f4b4bebdd9f6" :step/content "assigned-problem-79110b9e-fd73-5efb-8ce0-acef8691262c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186108823 :answer-field/choices 17592186108824] [:db/retract 17592186108823 :answer-field/choices 17592186108825] [:db/retract 17592186108823 :answer-field/choices 17592186108826] [:db/retract 17592186108823 :answer-field/choices 17592186108827] [:db/retract 17592186108823 :answer-field/choices 17592186108828] {:db/id 17592186110612 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-49274b7d-2639-522a-95c1-022f42cefd65" "question-eed297ec-4147-52da-9849-c80c4b4539e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110346 :question/problem "Solve $-2(3k-12)^2 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3k-12)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3k-12)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3k-12| & =0 \\\\ 3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =12 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110613 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9dc4d198-f79f-5ff0-bdf7-8f507199b081" "question-c1d7b383-1862-5cf5-aff4-6a1ed42adc9e" "question-b60332b2-78d9-552d-bf76-3110fd3f37e1" "question-98eaa55b-cc71-5317-b6aa-57c2d90c8064" "question-306bf2f9-520c-5fa5-bb34-4cf2b1fae3a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110348 :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(x+3)^2+10=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+10 & =k \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =k-10 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-10} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\sqrt{k-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k-10} & =0 \\\\ k-10 & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110614 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a2d7658e-2fb7-5107-b38d-4768d5865883" "question-511786af-e85b-5860-b040-e30ffe2050d6" "question-eec9dcf0-bc54-564e-8800-178ded8aabf7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110349 :question/problem "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{2}-18g+81 & =0 \\\\ {g}^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot g+{9}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (g-9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(g-9)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |g-9| & =0 \\\\ g-9 & =0 \\\\ g & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110615 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df298759-b302-529c-87f4-be55ef966373" "question-f96546d8-5860-5de5-ad05-79159b9add34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110350 :question/problem "Consider the equation $(4x+1)^2 +2=3k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" :question/worked-solution "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)^{2}+2 & =3k \\\\ (4x+1)^{2} & =3k-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{(4x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3k-2} \\\\ |4x+1| & =\\sqrt{3k-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n3k-2 < 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2 & <0 \\\\ 3k & <2 \\\\ k & <\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than $\\dfrac 2 3$ will give an equation with no real solutions." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111183 :question/problem "Solve $(2q+1)^{2}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2q+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(2q+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |2q+1| & =0 \\\\ 2q+1 & =0 \\\\ 2q & =-1 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108823 :answer-field/correct "answer-f0cad8d3-af30-578b-91c1-ab1f9fb13ed5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0cad8d3-af30-578b-91c1-ab1f9fb13ed5" "answer-24859fd2-c1b2-5831-8e51-fb1466c128e4" "answer-f263fd6b-0416-5c5a-a3b8-d6abab2d6179" "answer-fe3dee97-f98b-5e4b-b74d-89fe2dc202cc" "answer-855b1c7e-4914-5f65-94b2-e15147369927"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0cad8d3-af30-578b-91c1-ab1f9fb13ed5" :answer/id #uuid "f0cad8d3-af30-578b-91c1-ab1f9fb13ed5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24859fd2-c1b2-5831-8e51-fb1466c128e4" :answer/id #uuid "24859fd2-c1b2-5831-8e51-fb1466c128e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f263fd6b-0416-5c5a-a3b8-d6abab2d6179" :answer/id #uuid "f263fd6b-0416-5c5a-a3b8-d6abab2d6179" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe3dee97-f98b-5e4b-b74d-89fe2dc202cc" :answer/id #uuid "fe3dee97-f98b-5e4b-b74d-89fe2dc202cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-855b1c7e-4914-5f65-94b2-e15147369927" :answer/id #uuid "855b1c7e-4914-5f65-94b2-e15147369927" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-49274b7d-2639-522a-95c1-022f42cefd65" :question/id #uuid "49274b7d-2639-522a-95c1-022f42cefd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-222408" :question/problem "The solution to $-9(8a+2)^{2}=0$ is $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(8a+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (8a+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(8a+2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |8a+2| & =0 \\\\ 8a+2 & =0 \\\\ 8a & =-2 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5661bb64-4655-5bc6-81bf-0414e5f463d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5661bb64-4655-5bc6-81bf-0414e5f463d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "5661bb64-4655-5bc6-81bf-0414e5f463d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b89d7b54-d20e-5195-b3ec-558a112336ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b89d7b54-d20e-5195-b3ec-558a112336ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b89d7b54-d20e-5195-b3ec-558a112336ea" :answer/id #uuid "b89d7b54-d20e-5195-b3ec-558a112336ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eed297ec-4147-52da-9849-c80c4b4539e7" :question/id #uuid "eed297ec-4147-52da-9849-c80c4b4539e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-222406" :question/problem "The solution to $-6(3u-8)^{2}=0$ is $u=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(3u-8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3u-8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3u-8)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3u-8| & =0 \\\\ 3u-8 & =0 \\\\ 3u & =8 \\\\ u & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22b9b191-e1a0-51ff-9ce2-e94546c08ae8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22b9b191-e1a0-51ff-9ce2-e94546c08ae8" :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9b191-e1a0-51ff-9ce2-e94546c08ae8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-443f9ab6-a020-5cfa-8c4c-e19f45851927" :answer-field/choices ["answer-443f9ab6-a020-5cfa-8c4c-e19f45851927"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-443f9ab6-a020-5cfa-8c4c-e19f45851927" :answer/id #uuid "443f9ab6-a020-5cfa-8c4c-e19f45851927" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9dc4d198-f79f-5ff0-bdf7-8f507199b081" :question/id #uuid "9dc4d198-f79f-5ff0-bdf7-8f507199b081" :question/math-academy-id "q-111798" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation  ${x}^{2}+8=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k-8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-8} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-8 & =0 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=8$ will give only one solution to the equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2a6abe80-3c2d-572d-80a8-2e56881206aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a6abe80-3c2d-572d-80a8-2e56881206aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6abe80-3c2d-572d-80a8-2e56881206aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d014b300-40ba-556a-8389-c84731456411" "answer-259f42a6-bc99-586c-a899-1aec20f8acd7" "answer-903ed28d-ca35-56ad-8a32-039d529d866c" "answer-ea04fdc0-7467-527f-9fab-5c03cef45eeb" "answer-7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d014b300-40ba-556a-8389-c84731456411" :answer/id #uuid "d014b300-40ba-556a-8389-c84731456411" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-259f42a6-bc99-586c-a899-1aec20f8acd7" :answer/id #uuid "259f42a6-bc99-586c-a899-1aec20f8acd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-903ed28d-ca35-56ad-8a32-039d529d866c" :answer/id #uuid "903ed28d-ca35-56ad-8a32-039d529d866c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea04fdc0-7467-527f-9fab-5c03cef45eeb" :answer/id #uuid "ea04fdc0-7467-527f-9fab-5c03cef45eeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7" :answer/id #uuid "7fe9f2f0-3ee7-5eed-9878-14df14b06ff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1d7b383-1862-5cf5-aff4-6a1ed42adc9e" :question/id #uuid "c1d7b383-1862-5cf5-aff4-6a1ed42adc9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111797" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${t}^{2}+k-8=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+k-8 & =-2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-2-k+8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6-k} \\\\ |t| & =\\sqrt{6-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-k & =0 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6$ will give only one solution to the equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ff7defe-5d2a-5d0e-a412-4995a08ebb39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ff7defe-5d2a-5d0e-a412-4995a08ebb39" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff7defe-5d2a-5d0e-a412-4995a08ebb39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-782f1c18-62da-597f-ac6a-ecc309346025" "answer-fb3d8c4a-7d57-5058-9ead-bd693452cc53" "answer-c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" "answer-b16c83d5-f9f0-5284-959b-e164947c839e" "answer-aeaba178-c4f9-5f80-886d-7bfe5c72e6eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-782f1c18-62da-597f-ac6a-ecc309346025" :answer/id #uuid "782f1c18-62da-597f-ac6a-ecc309346025" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb3d8c4a-7d57-5058-9ead-bd693452cc53" :answer/id #uuid "fb3d8c4a-7d57-5058-9ead-bd693452cc53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" :answer/id #uuid "c05a90b1-3851-524e-b017-09d23e1cde34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b16c83d5-f9f0-5284-959b-e164947c839e" :answer/id #uuid "b16c83d5-f9f0-5284-959b-e164947c839e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeaba178-c4f9-5f80-886d-7bfe5c72e6eb" :answer/id #uuid "aeaba178-c4f9-5f80-886d-7bfe5c72e6eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b60332b2-78d9-552d-bf76-3110fd3f37e1" :question/id #uuid "b60332b2-78d9-552d-bf76-3110fd3f37e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-23862" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(1+3x)^{2}-5=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3x)^{2}-5 & =k \\\\ (1+3x)^{2} & =k+5 \\\\ \\sqrt{(1+3x)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+5} \\\\ |1+3x| & =\\sqrt{k+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k+5} & =0 \\\\ k+5 & =0 \\\\ k & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-5$ will give only one solution to the equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dff3bea7-288a-5339-8c3e-cff9c3c9f964"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dff3bea7-288a-5339-8c3e-cff9c3c9f964" :answer-field/id #uuid "dff3bea7-288a-5339-8c3e-cff9c3c9f964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24535b76-6948-59e2-9575-d8fc949658ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24535b76-6948-59e2-9575-d8fc949658ef" "answer-073b14de-c3fb-53d0-ba44-f3e01485ce49" "answer-973119e3-80d6-5f70-b10d-e87d31dd0758" "answer-80bb3dec-3e97-5f5b-a04e-48160805e362" "answer-0c485691-e6f8-54ae-90ca-cb01fde97789"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24535b76-6948-59e2-9575-d8fc949658ef" :answer/id #uuid "24535b76-6948-59e2-9575-d8fc949658ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-073b14de-c3fb-53d0-ba44-f3e01485ce49" :answer/id #uuid "073b14de-c3fb-53d0-ba44-f3e01485ce49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-973119e3-80d6-5f70-b10d-e87d31dd0758" :answer/id #uuid "973119e3-80d6-5f70-b10d-e87d31dd0758" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80bb3dec-3e97-5f5b-a04e-48160805e362" :answer/id #uuid "80bb3dec-3e97-5f5b-a04e-48160805e362" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c485691-e6f8-54ae-90ca-cb01fde97789" :answer/id #uuid "0c485691-e6f8-54ae-90ca-cb01fde97789" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-98eaa55b-cc71-5317-b6aa-57c2d90c8064" :question/id #uuid "98eaa55b-cc71-5317-b6aa-57c2d90c8064" :question/math-academy-id "q-44180" :question/problem "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}-1=k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+1} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =0 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-1$ will give only one solution to the equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29e6479f-97a4-5961-aec1-73e3ef1b6b66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29e6479f-97a4-5961-aec1-73e3ef1b6b66" :answer-field/id #uuid "29e6479f-97a4-5961-aec1-73e3ef1b6b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3085db40-e0a2-5354-b5dc-e8af7f89a863" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3085db40-e0a2-5354-b5dc-e8af7f89a863" "answer-c9fcb1ef-40eb-588c-b9d0-e648855f6cf6" "answer-b87d4bbd-b57c-52dd-aa35-a2a7822a6228" "answer-667d3d73-a100-5774-a023-55debdc7eb05" "answer-0c6f8269-d0f2-5032-b451-264bb83bc9fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3085db40-e0a2-5354-b5dc-e8af7f89a863" :answer/id #uuid "3085db40-e0a2-5354-b5dc-e8af7f89a863" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9fcb1ef-40eb-588c-b9d0-e648855f6cf6" :answer/id #uuid "c9fcb1ef-40eb-588c-b9d0-e648855f6cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b87d4bbd-b57c-52dd-aa35-a2a7822a6228" :answer/id #uuid "b87d4bbd-b57c-52dd-aa35-a2a7822a6228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-667d3d73-a100-5774-a023-55debdc7eb05" :answer/id #uuid "667d3d73-a100-5774-a023-55debdc7eb05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c6f8269-d0f2-5032-b451-264bb83bc9fb" :answer/id #uuid "0c6f8269-d0f2-5032-b451-264bb83bc9fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-306bf2f9-520c-5fa5-bb34-4cf2b1fae3a0" :question/id #uuid "306bf2f9-520c-5fa5-bb34-4cf2b1fae3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111795" :question/problem "Which value of $q$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}+q+3=7?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+q+3 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7-q-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4-q \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4-q} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{4-q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $q$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-q & =0 \\\\ q & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4$ will give only one solution to the equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5c722ad-44b6-5d52-bb78-d885d9d06d7e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5c722ad-44b6-5d52-bb78-d885d9d06d7e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c722ad-44b6-5d52-bb78-d885d9d06d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3edbdbd-ec90-5bcd-a793-beaa48e8be0e" "answer-041e724a-4ddb-5e58-90c2-60a8c0f44fee" "answer-1c93cda4-0807-53ea-a5d6-c1559bb51bc6" "answer-9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" "answer-9647c513-d055-5724-a06b-77925df0a593"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3edbdbd-ec90-5bcd-a793-beaa48e8be0e" :answer/id #uuid "b3edbdbd-ec90-5bcd-a793-beaa48e8be0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-041e724a-4ddb-5e58-90c2-60a8c0f44fee" :answer/id #uuid "041e724a-4ddb-5e58-90c2-60a8c0f44fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c93cda4-0807-53ea-a5d6-c1559bb51bc6" :answer/id #uuid "1c93cda4-0807-53ea-a5d6-c1559bb51bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" :answer/id #uuid "9e5201a0-d283-524b-8d15-3fcaaaa19246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9647c513-d055-5724-a06b-77925df0a593" :answer/id #uuid "9647c513-d055-5724-a06b-77925df0a593" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2d7658e-2fb7-5107-b38d-4768d5865883" :question/id #uuid "a2d7658e-2fb7-5107-b38d-4768d5865883" :question/math-academy-id "q-222450" :question/problem "If $4{y}^{2}+12y+9=0,$ then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}+12y+9 & =0 \\\\ (2y)^{2}+2\\cdot 2y\\cdot 3+{3}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2y+3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(2y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |2y+3| & =0 \\\\ 2y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90de0081-dc0f-59fe-a632-2b84f638ba91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90de0081-dc0f-59fe-a632-2b84f638ba91" :answer-field/id #uuid "90de0081-dc0f-59fe-a632-2b84f638ba91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-467430d2-d07a-5c3b-b1a7-dc892ea22db4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-467430d2-d07a-5c3b-b1a7-dc892ea22db4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-467430d2-d07a-5c3b-b1a7-dc892ea22db4" :answer/id #uuid "467430d2-d07a-5c3b-b1a7-dc892ea22db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-511786af-e85b-5860-b040-e30ffe2050d6" :question/id #uuid "511786af-e85b-5860-b040-e30ffe2050d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-111848" :question/problem "Given that  $9-24r+16{r}^{2}=0$, find $r.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-24r+16{r}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {3}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4r+(4r)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3-4r)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3-4r)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3-4r| & =0 \\\\ 3-4r & =0 \\\\ 4r & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00d3e51d-effc-5c49-b42a-b048eac125c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00d3e51d-effc-5c49-b42a-b048eac125c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "00d3e51d-effc-5c49-b42a-b048eac125c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31591f02-2246-5a37-997d-d58956049390" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7ff49b3-1eb9-5901-a7b9-6411b1d2d03e" "answer-31591f02-2246-5a37-997d-d58956049390" "answer-5d34b7a2-4375-5f19-a991-91ab0913d1e9" "answer-7fe183aa-8a1f-5e70-a54d-6fbbad2e2de5" "answer-b54ea052-39c4-55c3-b032-d8c1109973b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ff49b3-1eb9-5901-a7b9-6411b1d2d03e" :answer/id #uuid "e7ff49b3-1eb9-5901-a7b9-6411b1d2d03e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31591f02-2246-5a37-997d-d58956049390" :answer/id #uuid "31591f02-2246-5a37-997d-d58956049390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d34b7a2-4375-5f19-a991-91ab0913d1e9" :answer/id #uuid "5d34b7a2-4375-5f19-a991-91ab0913d1e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe183aa-8a1f-5e70-a54d-6fbbad2e2de5" :answer/id #uuid "7fe183aa-8a1f-5e70-a54d-6fbbad2e2de5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b54ea052-39c4-55c3-b032-d8c1109973b0" :answer/id #uuid "b54ea052-39c4-55c3-b032-d8c1109973b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eec9dcf0-bc54-564e-8800-178ded8aabf7" :question/id #uuid "eec9dcf0-bc54-564e-8800-178ded8aabf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-111841" :question/problem "Given that $9{t}^{2}+30t+25=0,$ what are the values of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{2}+30t+25 & =0 \\\\ (3t)^{2}+2\\cdot 3t\\cdot 5+{5}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3t+5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3t+5)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3t+5| & =0 \\\\ 3t+5 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =-5 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71992ead-ebe0-5657-9851-46873c0fbeed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71992ead-ebe0-5657-9851-46873c0fbeed" :answer-field/id #uuid "71992ead-ebe0-5657-9851-46873c0fbeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c666e615-c02f-56aa-b316-8da522b6eea5" "answer-f0ce4430-b6d5-5611-8ce7-55aa1ae50344" "answer-49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" "answer-eff16410-8ee2-5b35-9cd1-86c63ed51d55" "answer-4fab41a6-0bdb-57b1-8a90-0088e0974be6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c666e615-c02f-56aa-b316-8da522b6eea5" :answer/id #uuid "c666e615-c02f-56aa-b316-8da522b6eea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0ce4430-b6d5-5611-8ce7-55aa1ae50344" :answer/id #uuid "f0ce4430-b6d5-5611-8ce7-55aa1ae50344" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" :answer/id #uuid "49353a8c-8802-52d1-9ed3-d0af365872e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eff16410-8ee2-5b35-9cd1-86c63ed51d55" :answer/id #uuid "eff16410-8ee2-5b35-9cd1-86c63ed51d55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{2}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fab41a6-0bdb-57b1-8a90-0088e0974be6" :answer/id #uuid "4fab41a6-0bdb-57b1-8a90-0088e0974be6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df298759-b302-529c-87f4-be55ef966373" :question/id #uuid "df298759-b302-529c-87f4-be55ef966373" :question/math-academy-id "q-222457" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${v}^{2}-4a=16.$ For which values of $a$ does this equation have no real solutions?\n\n$a$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}-4a & =16 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16+4a \\\\ \\sqrt{{v}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16+4a} \\\\ |v| & =\\sqrt{16+4a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n16+4a<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+4a & <0 \\\\ 4a & <-16 \\\\ a & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $a$ that is less than $-4$ will give an equation with no real solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-451198a5-5570-555a-8151-6082d5376447" "answer-field-a220450f-0c54-5533-bc4f-ff97444fb758"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-451198a5-5570-555a-8151-6082d5376447" :answer-field/id #uuid "451198a5-5570-555a-8151-6082d5376447" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60a41429-39f2-58c1-b560-95db73d80dcb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60a41429-39f2-58c1-b560-95db73d80dcb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60a41429-39f2-58c1-b560-95db73d80dcb" :answer/id #uuid "60a41429-39f2-58c1-b560-95db73d80dcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a220450f-0c54-5533-bc4f-ff97444fb758" :answer-field/id #uuid "a220450f-0c54-5533-bc4f-ff97444fb758" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-08b0f9a3-96fa-5db2-be80-838f8d7a19d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08b0f9a3-96fa-5db2-be80-838f8d7a19d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08b0f9a3-96fa-5db2-be80-838f8d7a19d8" :answer/id #uuid "08b0f9a3-96fa-5db2-be80-838f8d7a19d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f96546d8-5860-5de5-ad05-79159b9add34" :question/id #uuid "f96546d8-5860-5de5-ad05-79159b9add34" :question/math-academy-id "q-23911" :question/problem "Consider the equation $(2x+2)^{2}-1=k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" :question/worked-solution "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+2)^{2}-1 & =k \\\\ (2x+2)^{2} & =k+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(2x+2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+1} \\\\ |2x+2| & =\\sqrt{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\nk+1<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & <0 \\\\ k & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $a$ that is less than $-1$ will give an equation with no real solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff637731-0d93-57b2-be2d-889df8bae7ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff637731-0d93-57b2-be2d-889df8bae7ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff637731-0d93-57b2-be2d-889df8bae7ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cb4bfac1-0fa6-50fb-944a-b417f8463b45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb4bfac1-0fa6-50fb-944a-b417f8463b45" "answer-9beae6c8-4ae7-5b4b-b857-6cd17d79aa64" "answer-6262ae6d-1e83-5f5c-9c22-b793d6eb9066" "answer-dc441aac-487b-544c-825d-7ea074fcd246" "answer-8a602158-e399-5555-aefb-b46bdb30dffa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb4bfac1-0fa6-50fb-944a-b417f8463b45" :answer/id #uuid "cb4bfac1-0fa6-50fb-944a-b417f8463b45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9beae6c8-4ae7-5b4b-b857-6cd17d79aa64" :answer/id #uuid "9beae6c8-4ae7-5b4b-b857-6cd17d79aa64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6262ae6d-1e83-5f5c-9c22-b793d6eb9066" :answer/id #uuid "6262ae6d-1e83-5f5c-9c22-b793d6eb9066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc441aac-487b-544c-825d-7ea074fcd246" :answer/id #uuid "dc441aac-487b-544c-825d-7ea074fcd246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a602158-e399-5555-aefb-b46bdb30dffa" :answer/id #uuid "8a602158-e399-5555-aefb-b46bdb30dffa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111919 :tutorial/content "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3x-6)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3x-6| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0.$ \n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x-6| & =0 \\\\ 3x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111920 :tutorial/content "Let's try and solve the equation $(x+1)^2=-1$ using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{-1} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\sqrt{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110817 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203334] [:db/retract 17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203335] [:db/retract 17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203336] [:db/retract 17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203337] [:db/retract 17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203371] [:db/retract 17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203372] [:db/retract 17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203373] [:db/retract 17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203374] [:db/retract 17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203375] {:db/id 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" "question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" "question-35662c2c-d0a2-5e1c-b319-cae7bd255ca0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203857 :question/problem "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at ![](images/70/70a078e9f26d91a1beb588e88c812057b85d31586576c94eaa510dedba7da2f0.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n![](images/26/26b51d20ec90168f9bf7602ed9e5f05cb4a5fb1421bb52fd0042ff81faa5f8ab.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where ![](images/db/dbd3e061ec5b3c6e148c347b0bf829391218f883ba4e6b1271bf8f65fb08d38b.svg), we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/79/790980ee64747a4ef3becd801431083d66839972cb271207807c43c53cf166fa.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2})[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2(-5-(-6))[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204080 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-55449077-495e-5404-a705-ef8294705226" "question-46d18a08-f2dc-5cb8-81b8-c0b6c16aa5ec" "question-86922573-d8de-5807-b0e8-74fee2201c80" "question-26793c1a-40f5-5bd2-9877-7d31b77eb2ea" "question-91187964-9e5b-5ba1-ba20-ee13e95e1abc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203871 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ where \n![](images/1d/1ddd8273660c1a84671e442af51d78ecc3df160fc0b807695c12297300118aa8.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at \n![](images/c6/c66b8dc8fde116a844d833de35034c534b650a9b7f788eef8366e770f6923380.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} y_i x_j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values ![](images/d8/d8a406621f1e60112d233f79cdde2e1405681554510ac4ea6af68409b4af4676.svg) and ![](images/df/dfa340dc46ddf622d0a16fdf8335a5183786294d395201e48f9adee4182bd91d.svg) we get\n\n![](images/b6/b6250171f2d99fb806cbb449b18f3a9c0513d8eac898d256d020e0b2cb986f8b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204081 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d4b3e437-844d-5303-9430-632a3c10c221" "question-77050388-523c-51b2-a4cd-6bc076f8c7c6" "question-def3b7cd-4317-5941-a403-30fe46331281" "question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203866 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}   \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}),$ where\n![](images/51/518bfea30c779d3e9dc1ab6b77b0ddaa9b938b56b0bb3b9e68d6c9b9cdae5666.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at \n![](images/a1/a1e2b39a295cebfd8425e8ab73e4d915233ca3afbd4141c54332c38f1091be15.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nWe can compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Then, $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} L = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y} \\bigg) = \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\nWriting out $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 (a_{ij} x_j - b_i),\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = x_j$ if $i = k$, and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = 0$ if $i \\neq k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ is the $i$th entry of $\\mathbf{y}$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $\\mathbf{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at ![](images/ee/ee9b9f891c92caf4229e8e92c20031fe7ba9e1cc0472105caf5a6cfaa8541a27.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204564 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{4-1} & \\frac{1}{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4-1} & -\\frac{2}{4-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203333 :answer-field/correct "answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" "answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" "answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" "answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/id #uuid "dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/id #uuid "23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/id #uuid "efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/id #uuid "3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204572 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4) \\\\ 2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$