} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" "answer-field-0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" "answer-field-f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer/id #uuid "d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer/id #uuid "f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512" :answer-field/id #uuid "f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer/id #uuid "df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/id #uuid "e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/math-academy-id "q-269472" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" "answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" "answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" "answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/id #uuid "5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/id #uuid "847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/id #uuid "e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/id #uuid "ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204585 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(3Ax-2b)^{T}(3Ax-2b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$-96$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$864$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=3Ax-2b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=3Ax-2b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=3{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-2{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 3{x}_{j} \\\\  & =6{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $6$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=6y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =6y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3Ax-2b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 132 \\\\ 216\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -96 \\\\ 0 & 528 \\\\ 0 & 864\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203439 :answer-field/correct "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/id #uuid "1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203438 :answer-field/correct "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/id #uuid "ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "528" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203440 :answer-field/correct "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/id #uuid "6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3ce184c982fe85eb25f79161431b7620227b96909d87998fa82efbb469423c.svg)\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A.$ It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n![](images/67/67c8cc92409c6bd1595fda93c8711ce91a40d220526cc52ee9a8f4d3842f7354.svg)\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}.\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}).\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & -\\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(X)}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n![](images/93/93200d9d52e6a58a52e4b9f65db6bc13ded0c6e17dbb8fc0381ae40081932ff9.svg)\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6-5 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output $f(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))$ would change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise. \n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l.\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left( \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n![](images/d1/d14a8e7752fdc2f20c9c325c5a0076d79a86ccd2a0c46d6b353ce53c3f1561d7.svg)\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n![](images/76/761731b884eee7282e01bda6964861fc8f3d4a3a44bfec09fda478170982b56a.svg)\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204182 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f69ba2b0-76ef-5087-a5bf-906e0adf24a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f69ba2b0-76ef-5087-a5bf-906e0adf24a4" :question/id #uuid "f69ba2b0-76ef-5087-a5bf-906e0adf24a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334388" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-6t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=6t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-6t)={e}^{-6t} \\\\ r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t} \\\\ 6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-12t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ac691a2-04d5-52ef-bc49-8d3327cd84e3" "answer-field-1ec158fd-889b-5066-b795-fdf52e06926c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ac691a2-04d5-52ef-bc49-8d3327cd84e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac691a2-04d5-52ef-bc49-8d3327cd84e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4" :answer/id #uuid "f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ec158fd-889b-5066-b795-fdf52e06926c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec158fd-889b-5066-b795-fdf52e06926c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9" :answer/id #uuid "3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8" :question/id #uuid "20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336094" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8b3efd3-b7c3-58e8-b4b9-9e7263c863ee" "answer-field-20fff794-00dd-50a8-9256-5d3d679c4c80" "answer-field-99e500e9-2419-5436-86f7-bd0983f12961" "answer-field-047d2299-ba3b-5217-bd76-392b196edadf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8b3efd3-b7c3-58e8-b4b9-9e7263c863ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b3efd3-b7c3-58e8-b4b9-9e7263c863ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0a59e584-278d-59db-b6ea-7b7977de0f71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0a59e584-278d-59db-b6ea-7b7977de0f71" "answer-79c8042c-d474-5943-aed4-1c689601cb7c" "answer-b483bb15-790a-5e86-a64e-5ba317eecac7" "answer-cc4405cf-0772-5f28-9f99-4a05e4a8fb85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "ans