answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bae1a6b-5b4f-5d7e-bafc-72b4dd45bce6" :answer/id #uuid "4bae1a6b-5b4f-5d7e-bafc-72b4dd45bce6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The largest value of $h(x)$ on $[-1,1].$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d18115-9663-5052-9c07-22d8d804669d" :answer/id #uuid "21d18115-9663-5052-9c07-22d8d804669d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The smallest value of $h(x)$ on $[-1,1].$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-031d6483-a285-51fe-8ffd-764744e050a2" :answer/id #uuid "031d6483-a285-51fe-8ffd-764744e050a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The value of $x$ in $[-1,1]$ where $h(x)$ is largest." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80db74a6-c35f-567d-a346-735228c01219" :answer/id #uuid "80db74a6-c35f-567d-a346-735228c01219" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The value of $x$ in $[-1,1]$ where $h(x)$ is smallest." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" :question/id #uuid "0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-355401" :question/problem "Let $w(y)$ be a weight for node $y,$ where $y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}.$ We want to write the selected node $\\overset{^}{y}$ as \"the value of $y$ that makes $w(y)$ as large as possible.\"\n\nWhich of the following correctly defines $\\overset{^}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "We want $\\overset{^}{y}$ to be an *input value* $y$ where the weight $w(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" "answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" "answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" "answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" "answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/id #uuid "eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/id #uuid "450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/id #uuid "e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in w(y)}\\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/id #uuid "a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/id #uuid "76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186232041 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we used $\\max$ and $\\min$ to talk about the largest and smallest *values* a function can take on a set.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn two closely related pieces of notation:\n\n- $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the best (largest/smallest) *function value*.\n\n- $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the *input value* (the \"argument\") where that best value occurs.\n\nFor example, let $S = \\{1,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nf(1)=4,\\qquad f(2)=7,\\qquad f(3)=6.\n$$\n\nThen the largest function value is $7,$ and it occurs when $x=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =7, \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest function value, and $\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input where that smallest value occurs.\n\n**Watch out!** Sometimes the best value occurs at more than one input. In that case, we need to clarify what $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$) means. We will handle this after we practice the basic case." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186232042 :tutorial/content "Sometimes a function attains its largest (or smallest) value at more than one input. In that case, there are multiple inputs that satisfy the definition of $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$).\n\nIn this course, when a tie occurs, we report the *set* of all inputs where the maximum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\max_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when a tie occurs, we report the set of all inputs where the minimum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\min_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nThe quantities $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ are still single numbers." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231551 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186094771 :answer-field/choices 17592186094772] [:db/retract 17592186094771 :answer-field/choices 17592186094773] [:db/retract 17592186094771 :answer-field/choices 17592186094774] [:db/retract 17592186094771 :answer-field/choices 17592186094775] [:db/retract 17592186094771 :answer-field/choices 17592186094776] {:db/id 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" "question-fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" "question-65750eba-d000-52c0-9873-7b35cf42c92d" "question-758e2016-fc3b-5023-8258-63b37b29bb3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096561 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3,\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{3}(-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{4}{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left( -2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae8f5be4-7a5b-5146-8905-098ca73c2947" "question-26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" "question-9ef740c3-9ce9-5fcc-84b0-6a5ac9ae7a65" "question-c3acb4f5-8405-55f7-be88-53d3f71a5599" "question-7fa9ec9c-84e1-5ae9-97e5-a5fa49b7d98b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096538 :question/problem "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({t}^{3}-4t)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [-2,0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{t}^{2}-{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-2\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{8}{3})-2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" :question/id #uuid "28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-21261" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a886f775367f244f78087935cb717b988d85f1714b2cae7ce3e4ba2525ef178d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-272dc5e4-a05a-579c-866f-fc7b62dce493"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-272dc5e4-a05a-579c-866f-fc7b62dce493" :answer-field/id #uuid "272dc5e4-a05a-579c-866f-fc7b62dce493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2be295bf-ab71-5c18-a2d8-e9a46caf6c7e" "answer-b2047660-6762-59c0-8c44-62f24bd512e0" "answer-4a25f513-1d63-5b75-9076-d74d78ecfa96" "answer-d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" "answer-48e2bc52-9d10-5087-adba-a5e457f49069"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2be295bf-ab71-5c18-a2d8-e9a46caf6c7e" :answer/id #uuid "2be295bf-ab71-5c18-a2d8-e9a46caf6c7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2047660-6762-59c0-8c44-62f24bd512e0" :answer/id #uuid "b2047660-6762-59c0-8c44-62f24bd512e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a25f513-1d63-5b75-9076-d74d78ecfa96" :answer/id #uuid "4a25f513-1d63-5b75-9076-d74d78ecfa96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" :answer/id #uuid "d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48e2bc52-9d10-5087-adba-a5e457f49069" :answer/id #uuid "48e2bc52-9d10-5087-adba-a5e457f49069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" :question/id #uuid "fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15703" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{sec}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b75b49fc-3a38-5d99-88f4-84100e658eca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b75b49fc-3a38-5d99-88f4-84100e658eca" :answer-field/id #uuid "b75b49fc-3a38-5d99-88f4-84100e658eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b62d29f0-effc-5844-9775-69af319ada0d" "answer-bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" "answer-05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" "answer-bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" "answer-35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b62d29f0-effc-5844-9775-69af319ada0d" :answer/id #uuid "b62d29f0-effc-5844-9775-69af319ada0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" :answer/id #uuid "bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" :answer/id #uuid "05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" :answer/id #uuid "bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/id #uuid "35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65750eba-d000-52c0-9873-7b35cf42c92d" :question/id #uuid "65750eba-d000-52c0-9873-7b35cf42c92d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15697" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)=3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4bc41e98-bdd4-510c-bb8f-f153e9da4aad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4bc41e98-bdd4-510c-bb8f-f153e9da4aad" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc41e98-bdd4-510c-bb8f-f153e9da4aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5ed7e91-3d72-56b5-ab62-5c3e1bc6ce84" "answer-b0d8a8f7-9728-574b-a5bf-93dc33ae91c7" "answer-a97f4b2f-c728-5cb6-86b1-cfb0247b8d9f" "answer-96ccd538-a21d-543b-84e0-95a8be106823" "answer-a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5ed7e91-3d72-56b5-ab62-5c3e1bc6ce84" :answer/id #uuid "b5ed7e91-3d72-56b5-ab62-5c3e1bc6ce84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0d8a8f7-9728-574b-a5bf-93dc33ae91c7" :answer/id #uuid "b0d8a8f7-9728-574b-a5bf-93dc33ae91c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a97f4b2f-c728-5cb6-86b1-cfb0247b8d9f" :answer/id #uuid "a97f4b2f-c728-5cb6-86b1-cfb0247b8d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96ccd538-a21d-543b-84e0-95a8be106823" :answer/id #uuid "96ccd538-a21d-543b-84e0-95a8be106823" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5" :answer/id #uuid "a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-758e2016-fc3b-5023-8258-63b37b29bb3a" :question/id #uuid "758e2016-fc3b-5023-8258-63b37b29bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15702" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-282f87a1-fbff-540e-9588-15f337435902"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-282f87a1-fbff-540e-9588-15f337435902" :answer-field/id #uuid "282f87a1-fbff-540e-9588-15f337435902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ab8b9c7-33ef-5efc-9e7b-b5a66785ef0a" "answer-7d09ac4b-ab8c-5486-8d70-86fa00fbd80f" "answer-a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" "answer-dc79816e-db4c-5c6c-9732-8d3c834824bd" "answer-68b23a53-a860-5499-be46-e095e504ab4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ab8b9c7-33ef-5efc-9e7b-b5a66785ef0a" :answer/id #uuid "9ab8b9c7-33ef-5efc-9e7b-b5a66785ef0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d09ac4b-ab8c-5486-8d70-86fa00fbd80f" :answer/id #uuid "7d09ac4b-ab8c-5486-8d70-86fa00fbd80f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" :answer/id #uuid "a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc79816e-db4c-5c6c-9732-8d3c834824bd" :answer/id #uuid "dc79816e-db4c-5c6c-9732-8d3c834824bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68b23a53-a860-5499-be46-e095e504ab4b" :answer/id #uuid "68b23a53-a860-5499-be46-e095e504ab4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097133 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)4t({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186094771 :answer-field/correct "answer-0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc32fe65-23f4-585b-b44d-bc65d429edc6" "answer-4e769969-8957-55a2-b6ab-5d02f6fc2146" "answer-bcaf9820-1bec-5a03-9a35-ed2b702da002" "answer-ad138fd0-cf17-507e-a68b-3714864985e9" "answer-0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc32fe65-23f4-585b-b44d-bc65d429edc6" :answer/id #uuid "dc32fe65-23f4-585b-b44d-bc65d429edc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-t)({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e769969-8957-55a2-b6ab-5d02f6fc2146" :answer/id #uuid "4e769969-8957-55a2-b6ab-5d02f6fc2146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcaf9820-1bec-5a03-9a35-ed2b702da002" :answer/id #uuid "bcaf9820-1bec-5a03-9a35-ed2b702da002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad138fd0-cf17-507e-a68b-3714864985e9" :answer/id #uuid "ad138fd0-cf17-507e-a68b-3714864985e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-t)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091" :answer/id #uuid "0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae8f5be4-7a5b-5146-8905-098ca73c2947" :question/id #uuid "ae8f5be4-7a5b-5146-8905-098ca73c2947" :question/math-academy-id "q-351633" :question/problem "![](images/09/0928371c060dc402198b610e7118fdcff68cd0e10c54df43931ff82d2a2697b4.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ and $t\\ge 0.$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi (1)-\\pi (-1))\\,| \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-655bb155-c367-5d19-b7ad-147108ed79e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-655bb155-c367-5d19-b7ad-147108ed79e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "655bb155-c367-5d19-b7ad-147108ed79e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1" :answer/id #uuid "872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" :question/id #uuid "26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" :question/math-academy-id "q-10208" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0d11e89592cad64ebbeb72def6ca0a6c5880a9a54f82b394d7857e142c05edf.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-1)(3{t}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-7{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{1}-7\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1}+4\\cdot t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{5}-0)-7(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{7}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a3d8b06-7a62-51cc-b468-4393734ce6fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a3d8b06-7a62-51cc-b468-4393734ce6fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a3d8b06-7a62-51cc-b468-4393734ce6fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6b49b5a-7449-5ccb-b7ed-94a7b282e585" "answer-4fc9aebc-6c01-51de-a0b0-7677f18cc47d" "answer-0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" "answer-1c30baac-673c-57e4-bb6d-271c0fec004e" "answer-41ebf8a0-45bd-57a0-9e78-b6a77b454d60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6b49b5a-7449-5ccb-b7ed-94a7b282e585" :answer/id #uuid "f6b49b5a-7449-5ccb-b7ed-94a7b282e585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fc9aebc-6c01-51de-a0b0-7677f18cc47d" :answer/id #uuid "4fc9aebc-6c01-51de-a0b0-7677f18cc47d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" :answer/id #uuid "0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{34}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c30baac-673c-57e4-bb6d-271c0fec004e" :answer/id #uuid "1c30baac-673c-57e4-bb6d-271c0fec004e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41ebf8a0-45bd-57a0-9e78-b6a77b454d60" :answer/id #uuid "41ebf8a0-45bd-57a0-9e78-b6a77b454d60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ef740c3-9ce9-5fcc-84b0-6a5ac9ae7a65" :question/id #uuid "9ef740c3-9ce9-5fcc-84b0-6a5ac9ae7a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-10214" :question/problem "![](images/16/1628d71e973666b1b79aa5e86a9fa4b7cc8fffec93778bde92db7b3600bd13f5.png)\n\nFind the area enclosed by the curve (shown above) defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,y={t}^{3}-4t,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)=2t.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{3}-4t)\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2}-8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{32}{5})-8(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{128}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is $|-\\frac{256}{15}|=\\frac{256}{15}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ba96fc9-73a2-5b2b-9e01-ee5a33e187b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ba96fc9-73a2-5b2b-9e01-ee5a33e187b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba96fc9-73a2-5b2b-9e01-ee5a33e187b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6cc00630-1342-5bbe-ab51-a97a4c091b46" "answer-5d6d8667-2807-5f6b-8f49-adf93ef75072" "answer-28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" "answer-d720d6b2-51cb-5455-acd7-e39b10f0feef" "answer-530f3b53-a178-5021-8567-be1e8771a0d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cc00630-1342-5bbe-ab51-a97a4c091b46" :answer/id #uuid "6cc00630-1342-5bbe-ab51-a97a4c091b46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{128}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d6d8667-2807-5f6b-8f49-adf93ef75072" :answer/id #uuid "5d6d8667-2807-5f6b-8f49-adf93ef75072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" :answer/id #uuid "28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d720d6b2-51cb-5455-acd7-e39b10f0feef" :answer/id #uuid "d720d6b2-51cb-5455-acd7-e39b10f0feef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{512}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-530f3b53-a178-5021-8567-be1e8771a0d9" :answer/id #uuid "530f3b53-a178-5021-8567-be1e8771a0d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3acb4f5-8405-55f7-be88-53d3f71a5599" :question/id #uuid "c3acb4f5-8405-55f7-be88-53d3f71a5599" :question/math-academy-id "q-351638" :question/problem "![](images/60/60f01450ff74239f3f9bedcf9dfc4445f9c557bb52584ee79a0ba61b0d8bb6f7.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)-(t+1)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2)-(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e6ff139-6e60-549e-b50b-521438ae9cc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e6ff139-6e60-549e-b50b-521438ae9cc8" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6ff139-6e60-549e-b50b-521438ae9cc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e" :answer/id #uuid "0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\ln 2-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fa9ec9c-84e1-5ae9-97e5-a5fa49b7d98b" :question/id #uuid "7fa9ec9c-84e1-5ae9-97e5-a5fa49b7d98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-21273" :question/problem "![](images/39/399578c2a4d43e64be3f811ab0fdf0f5a62456c29d77dc3a822cc0ecea733c77.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2},\\,y=2t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f1a9067-bb2d-5d4e-be8a-b58dfed87758"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f1a9067-bb2d-5d4e-be8a-b58dfed87758" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f1a9067-bb2d-5d4e-be8a-b58dfed87758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44a447b9-c086-5162-9309-62ffa3961404" "answer-2def6620-57ba-5d95-9beb-b3aeecd0552a" "answer-db98e4f8-00f3-5545-84b9-5d5a5f5d9362" "answer-f6553b7c-f441-5101-85f6-7f1945901c38" "answer-c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44a447b9-c086-5162-9309-62ffa3961404" :answer/id #uuid "44a447b9-c086-5162-9309-62ffa3961404" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2def6620-57ba-5d95-9beb-b3aeecd0552a" :answer/id #uuid "2def6620-57ba-5d95-9beb-b3aeecd0552a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db98e4f8-00f3-5545-84b9-5d5a5f5d9362" :answer/id #uuid "db98e4f8-00f3-5545-84b9-5d5a5f5d9362" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6553b7c-f441-5101-85f6-7f1945901c38" :answer/id #uuid "f6553b7c-f441-5101-85f6-7f1945901c38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124" :answer/id #uuid "c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097798 :tutorial/content "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad\ny=2t-t^3, \\qquad\nt \\in  (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1,$ as shown. \n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y(t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{3}-3{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097915 :tutorial/content "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! \n\nTo see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0.$ However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0\n$$\n\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096851 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097612 :step/math-academy-id 630055 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097747 :step/math-academy-id 630056 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097653 :step/math-academy-id 630057 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097613 :step/math-academy-id 630060 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499441 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cceba1db-cf64-5613-ace7-49dabd9ac195" "question-987f037f-66db-5b9e-acaa-f2176b3629c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cceba1db-cf64-5613-ace7-49dabd9ac195" :question/id #uuid "cceba1db-cf64-5613-ace7-49dabd9ac195" :question/math-academy-id "q-93076" :question/problem "Only apple and cherry trees grow in a garden. The following table shows the number of these trees.\n\n| Apple trees | Cherry trees |\n| --- | --- |\n| $271$ | $425$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the number of apple trees to the total number of trees." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of apple and cherry trees by rounding to the nearest hundred.\n\n- Apple trees: $271⟶300$\n\n- Cherry trees: $425⟶400$\n\nThus, the total number of trees is approximately $300+400=700.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of apple trees to the total number of trees (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{300}{700}.\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{300÷100}{700÷100}=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of apple trees to the total number of trees is approximately $\\frac{3}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69081031-c0ac-5dc2-9f83-909e212c6666"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69081031-c0ac-5dc2-9f83-909e212c6666" :answer-field/id #uuid "69081031-c0ac-5dc2-9f83-909e212c6666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7525cca7-6301-5a3f-84e3-2eb780c2eb28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-02603c07-dd10-5687-84ae-5f4c0be4ab2c" "answer-28a8ea65-0785-5201-bbdf-14a3661d4e5a" "answer-6ca4d6ec-255a-5d79-af66-de6061ebddfb" "answer-3c64e603-d081-5cb3-9278-c26a4ab3e27e" "answer-7525cca7-6301-5a3f-84e3-2eb780c2eb28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02603c07-dd10-5687-84ae-5f4c0be4ab2c" :answer/id #uuid "02603c07-dd10-5687-84ae-5f4c0be4ab2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28a8ea65-0785-5201-bbdf-14a3661d4e5a" :answer/id #uuid "28a8ea65-0785-5201-bbdf-14a3661d4e5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ca4d6ec-255a-5d79-af66-de6061ebddfb" :answer/id #uuid "6ca4d6ec-255a-5d79-af66-de6061ebddfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c64e603-d081-5cb3-9278-c26a4ab3e27e" :answer/id #uuid "3c64e603-d081-5cb3-9278-c26a4ab3e27e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7525cca7-6301-5a3f-84e3-2eb780c2eb28" :answer/id #uuid "7525cca7-6301-5a3f-84e3-2eb780c2eb28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499434 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-de53ade3-2006-5489-a226-17f2c3957439" "question-f65ffc41-5bca-5c7e-a470-b626911bf2cd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-de53ade3-2006-5489-a226-17f2c3957439" :question/id #uuid "de53ade3-2006-5489-a226-17f2c3957439" :question/math-academy-id "q-93112" :question/problem "There are $98$ blue marbles and some red marbles in a box. The ratio of the number of blue marbles to the number of red ones is $2:1.$ By rounding the number of blue marbles to the nearest ten, approximate the number of red marbles in the box." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of blue marbles:\n\n$$\n98⟶100\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the number of blue marbles to the number of red marbles is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the number of blue marbles and the denominator represents the number of red marbles.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $100$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $50:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 50}{1\\times 50}=\\frac{100}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $50$ red marbles in the box." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d04ac50-0af2-5115-b945-5c001afd6b25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d04ac50-0af2-5115-b945-5c001afd6b25" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d04ac50-0af2-5115-b945-5c001afd6b25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b73f981-baed-51be-bc72-ae388cf830f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25cb37b5-2d4e-5670-bd41-bb1e65a2f188" "answer-28656814-767a-5b15-91aa-42642f0f926b" "answer-3b73f981-baed-51be-bc72-ae388cf830f1" "answer-5dffe28d-f55c-57d9-a24f-952a67152c73" "answer-a3cdbf4a-9fd3-52b6-ba6f-df93bb7df832"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25cb37b5-2d4e-5670-bd41-bb1e65a2f188" :answer/id #uuid "25cb37b5-2d4e-5670-bd41-bb1e65a2f188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28656814-767a-5b15-91aa-42642f0f926b" :answer/id #uuid "28656814-767a-5b15-91aa-42642f0f926b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b73f981-baed-51be-bc72-ae388cf830f1" :answer/id #uuid "3b73f981-baed-51be-bc72-ae388cf830f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dffe28d-f55c-57d9-a24f-952a67152c73" :answer/id #uuid "5dffe28d-f55c-57d9-a24f-952a67152c73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3cdbf4a-9fd3-52b6-ba6f-df93bb7df832" :answer/id #uuid "a3cdbf4a-9fd3-52b6-ba6f-df93bb7df832" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f65ffc41-5bca-5c7e-a470-b626911bf2cd" :question/id #uuid "f65ffc41-5bca-5c7e-a470-b626911bf2cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93113" :question/problem "Samantha and Harry each bought a watermelon. The ratio of the weight of Samantha's watermelon to the weight of Harry's watermelon is $5:3.$ Samantha's watermelon weighs $12$ kilograms. By rounding this weight to the nearest ten, approximate the weight of Harry's watermelon." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the weight of Samantha's watermelon to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n12⟶10\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the weight of Samantha's watermelon to the weight of Harry's watermelon is \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the weight of Samantha's  watermelon and the denominator represents  the weight of Harry's watermelon.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $10$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 2}{3\\times 2}=\\frac{10}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Harry's watermelon approximately weighs $6$ kilograms." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69d68b6c-b19c-5342-87f0-f1a951317aa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69d68b6c-b19c-5342-87f0-f1a951317aa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "69d68b6c-b19c-5342-87f0-f1a951317aa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c515615-ec9e-5ba1-8ca1-601c55e23e35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9360364-ef93-5795-aaa0-0811fa98160b" "answer-f8c87a01-7ace-5e64-a20d-4355299a734e" "answer-2e9d41c5-7037-5cd7-aa0c-6d98056a697a" "answer-0c3f15eb-c686-501d-80b8-1f41855dcc40" "answer-1c515615-ec9e-5ba1-8ca1-601c55e23e35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9360364-ef93-5795-aaa0-0811fa98160b" :answer/id #uuid "c9360364-ef93-5795-aaa0-0811fa98160b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10$ kilograms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8c87a01-7ace-5e64-a20d-4355299a734e" :answer/id #uuid "f8c87a01-7ace-5e64-a20d-4355299a734e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$15$ kilograms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e9d41c5-7037-5cd7-aa0c-6d98056a697a" :answer/id #uuid "2e9d41c5-7037-5cd7-aa0c-6d98056a697a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ kilograms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c3f15eb-c686-501d-80b8-1f41855dcc40" :answer/id #uuid "0c3f15eb-c686-501d-80b8-1f41855dcc40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ kilograms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c515615-ec9e-5ba1-8ca1-601c55e23e35" :answer/id #uuid "1c515615-ec9e-5ba1-8ca1-601c55e23e35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ kilograms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499440 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cc81aa2c-a8cb-5dba-82e1-77d0c6319f70"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc81aa2c-a8cb-5dba-82e1-77d0c6319f70" :question/id #uuid "cc81aa2c-a8cb-5dba-82e1-77d0c6319f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-93267" :question/problem "The following table shows the amount, in grams, of flax oil obtained from the corresponding amount of flaxseed:\n\n| Oil | Seed |\n| --- | --- |\n| $315$ | $945$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the amount of flax oil to the amount of flaxseed is always the same. By rounding the given data to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of flax oil that can be obtained from $600$ grams of seed." :question/worked-solution "First, let's round the amounts of flax oil and seed:\n\n- Oil: $315⟶300$\n\n- Seed: $945⟶900$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the amount of flax oil to the amount of flaxseed (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{300}{900}.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the amount of flax oil and the denominator represents the amount of flaxseed.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{300÷100}{900÷100}=\\frac{3}{9}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3÷3}{9÷3}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $600$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 200}{3\\times 200}=\\frac{200}{600}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $200$ grams of flax oil can be obtained from $600$ grams of flaxseed." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a8f5b83-2a82-5f6e-95fd-274ba7bd818d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a8f5b83-2a82-5f6e-95fd-274ba7bd818d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a8f5b83-2a82-5f6e-95fd-274ba7bd818d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-890baf66-3b7c-5d6b-a240-4e42025caf2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72bffca4-ed36-5955-afe3-b6ce9cb972ed" "answer-f1b144a7-2a2a-5401-8855-dfc1a8d02933" "answer-93031dca-4a2f-590b-b511-f9479c81542f" "answer-55cdd9f1-49bc-529f-9cf3-7d15b171045a" "answer-890baf66-3b7c-5d6b-a240-4e42025caf2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72bffca4-ed36-5955-afe3-b6ce9cb972ed" :answer/id #uuid "72bffca4-ed36-5955-afe3-b6ce9cb972ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$125$ grams" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1b144a7-2a2a-5401-8855-dfc1a8d02933" :answer/id #uuid "f1b144a7-2a2a-5401-8855-dfc1a8d02933" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$130$ grams" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93031dca-4a2f-590b-b511-f9479c81542f" :answer/id #uuid "93031dca-4a2f-590b-b511-f9479c81542f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$160$ grams" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55cdd9f1-49bc-529f-9cf3-7d15b171045a" :answer/id #uuid "55cdd9f1-49bc-529f-9cf3-7d15b171045a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$180$ grams" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-890baf66-3b7c-5d6b-a240-4e42025caf2d" :answer/id #uuid "890baf66-3b7c-5d6b-a240-4e42025caf2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$200$ grams" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-987f037f-66db-5b9e-acaa-f2176b3629c8" :question/id #uuid "987f037f-66db-5b9e-acaa-f2176b3629c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-349588" :question/problem "Only orange and peach trees grow in a garden. The following table shows the number of these trees.\n\n| Orange trees | Peach trees |\n| --- | --- |\n| $352$ | $618$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the number of orange trees to the total number of trees." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the number of orange and peach trees by rounding to the nearest $100$:\n\n- Orange trees: $352⟶400$\n\n- Peach trees: $618⟶600$\n\nThus, the total number of trees is approximately $400+600=1\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of orange trees to the total number of trees is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{400}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{400÷100}{1\\,000÷100}=\\frac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of orange trees to the total number of trees is approximately $\\frac{2}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94410187-a34e-500a-81d7-ff4c37da8f94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94410187-a34e-500a-81d7-ff4c37da8f94" :answer-field/id #uuid "94410187-a34e-500a-81d7-ff4c37da8f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d2c04c8e-bd71-5713-91f5-1e65209c705d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c06d78f0-10b4-518d-8220-6a66d562d55c" "answer-d2c04c8e-bd71-5713-91f5-1e65209c705d" "answer-f23155c7-a1e2-5198-af78-7d8df533c0b4" "answer-0c08cff7-c809-5121-b744-11d3019f8279" "answer-ce7add3f-d5d9-5c37-a278-9620c6062894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c06d78f0-10b4-518d-8220-6a66d562d55c" :answer/id #uuid "c06d78f0-10b4-518d-8220-6a66d562d55c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c04c8e-bd71-5713-91f5-1e65209c705d" :answer/id #uuid "d2c04c8e-bd71-5713-91f5-1e65209c705d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f23155c7-a1e2-5198-af78-7d8df533c0b4" :answer/id #uuid "f23155c7-a1e2-5198-af78-7d8df533c0b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c08cff7-c809-5121-b744-11d3019f8279" :answer/id #uuid "0c08cff7-c809-5121-b744-11d3019f8279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce7add3f-d5d9-5c37-a278-9620c6062894" :answer/id #uuid "ce7add3f-d5d9-5c37-a278-9620c6062894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-09a164ce-ad3d-58c3-9305-a48a254fe99f" :activity/id #uuid "09a164ce-ad3d-58c3-9305-a48a254fe99f" :activity/title "Approximating Ratios" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048840 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-a099c9b4-d1ce-5863-8969-e24fe375544d" "step-efa27ae5-ad45-5b59-a5b8-4d12f16c5865" "step-5ae6fd03-7d26-5fc4-8d37-964087984c67" "step-bf26c5b2-fa41-5f58-9926-3debfa64da79" "step-b89d46db-aacc-5300-81b4-f42199193213"] :activity/first-step "step-a099c9b4-d1ce-5863-8969-e24fe375544d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a099c9b4-d1ce-5863-8969-e24fe375544d" :step/id #uuid "a099c9b4-d1ce-5863-8969-e24fe375544d" :step/content "question-cceba1db-cf64-5613-ace7-49dabd9ac195" :step/next "step-efa27ae5-ad45-5b59-a5b8-4d12f16c5865" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-efa27ae5-ad45-5b59-a5b8-4d12f16c5865" :step/id #uuid "efa27ae5-ad45-5b59-a5b8-4d12f16c5865" :step/content "question-de53ade3-2006-5489-a226-17f2c3957439" :step/next "step-5ae6fd03-7d26-5fc4-8d37-964087984c67" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5ae6fd03-7d26-5fc4-8d37-964087984c67" :step/id #uuid "5ae6fd03-7d26-5fc4-8d37-964087984c67" :step/content "question-f65ffc41-5bca-5c7e-a470-b626911bf2cd" :step/next "step-bf26c5b2-fa41-5f58-9926-3debfa64da79" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bf26c5b2-fa41-5f58-9926-3debfa64da79" :step/id #uuid "bf26c5b2-fa41-5f58-9926-3debfa64da79" :step/content "question-cc81aa2c-a8cb-5dba-82e1-77d0c6319f70" :step/next "step-b89d46db-aacc-5300-81b4-f42199193213" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b89d46db-aacc-5300-81b4-f42199193213" :step/id #uuid "b89d46db-aacc-5300-81b4-f42199193213" :step/content "question-987f037f-66db-5b9e-acaa-f2176b3629c8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6d6e949e-2c23-5f49-968a-1d0b9baadd85"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d6e949e-2c23-5f49-968a-1d0b9baadd85" :question/id #uuid "6d6e949e-2c23-5f49-968a-1d0b9baadd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-115659" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+6}+\\frac{B}{(x+6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+11=A(x+6)^{2}+B(x+6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{2}+8(-6)+11 & =A(-6+6)^{2}+B(-6+6)+C \\\\ -1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+8(0)+11 & =A(0+6)^{2}+B(0+6)+C \\\\ 11 & =36A+6B-1 \\\\ 36A+6B & =12 \\\\ 6A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)^{2}+8(-5)+11 & =A(-5+6)^{2}+B(-5+6)+C \\\\ -4 & =A+B-1 \\\\ A+B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $6A+B=2$ and $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A & =2-(-3) \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-3 \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-47ba250e-6800-54ee-b70a-058d44137afb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-47ba250e-6800-54ee-b70a-058d44137afb" :answer-field/id #uuid "47ba250e-6800-54ee-b70a-058d44137afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d22e668-822b-5060-b1a5-9f37a1e7fced" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b1f2cc0-8a4e-5ea4-a72e-cd65516b5d90" "answer-972c848e-af74-5c16-9e1d-640235fc2342" "answer-d2d45bcb-56e5-5650-bf20-a80f6e1fdbca" "answer-a747ce04-2a78-5a1f-92c1-42721d3c067e" "answer-7d22e668-822b-5060-b1a5-9f37a1e7fced"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b1f2cc0-8a4e-5ea4-a72e-cd65516b5d90" :answer/id #uuid "4b1f2cc0-8a4e-5ea4-a72e-cd65516b5d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|-\\frac{1}{(x+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-972c848e-af74-5c16-9e1d-640235fc2342" :answer/id #uuid "972c848e-af74-5c16-9e1d-640235fc2342" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2d45bcb-56e5-5650-bf20-a80f6e1fdbca" :answer/id #uuid "d2d45bcb-56e5-5650-bf20-a80f6e1fdbca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{2}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a747ce04-2a78-5a1f-92c1-42721d3c067e" :answer/id #uuid "a747ce04-2a78-5a1f-92c1-42721d3c067e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x+6)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d22e668-822b-5060-b1a5-9f37a1e7fced" :answer/id #uuid "7d22e668-822b-5060-b1a5-9f37a1e7fced" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068800 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45" :question/id #uuid "4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346722" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n9-2x=A(x-3)^{2}+Bx(x-3)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(0) & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0)(0-3)+C(0) \\\\ 9 & =9A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(3) & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3)(3-3)+C(3) \\\\ 3 & =3C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(1) & =A(1-3)^{2}+B(1)(1-3)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =4A-2B+C \\\\ 7 & =4(1)-2B+1 \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5e49120-4a4d-5acc-b35e-098953907a6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5e49120-4a4d-5acc-b35e-098953907a6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e49120-4a4d-5acc-b35e-098953907a6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bc4ab644-7ab1-52fe-acea-608b6283d2d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc4ab644-7ab1-52fe-acea-608b6283d2d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc4ab644-7ab1-52fe-acea-608b6283d2d2" :answer/id #uuid "bc4ab644-7ab1-52fe-acea-608b6283d2d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\ln 2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068799 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d58d6a00-0092-54a9-834c-43383db09b35" "question-fd717c83-b2bc-5cde-b553-20c9ad00a74f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d58d6a00-0092-54a9-834c-43383db09b35" :question/id #uuid "d58d6a00-0092-54a9-834c-43383db09b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-348615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n5-3x=A(x+1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3(-1) & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3(0) & =A(0+1)+8 \\\\ 5 & =A+8 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{8}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{8}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-8(-1-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-782cb243-75dd-5b8b-940c-de9dd816a45d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-782cb243-75dd-5b8b-940c-de9dd816a45d" :answer-field/id #uuid "782cb243-75dd-5b8b-940c-de9dd816a45d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-30ec5fc0-ee34-584e-9d88-01e89c37816f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-30ec5fc0-ee34-584e-9d88-01e89c37816f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30ec5fc0-ee34-584e-9d88-01e89c37816f" :answer/id #uuid "30ec5fc0-ee34-584e-9d88-01e89c37816f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd717c83-b2bc-5cde-b553-20c9ad00a74f" :question/id #uuid "fd717c83-b2bc-5cde-b553-20c9ad00a74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348616" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-1=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)-1 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-1 & =A(0-2)+3 \\\\ -1 & =-2A+3 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(\\frac{2}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|\\,{|}_{3}^{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-3(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{1})-3(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dfd3ac59-843e-571d-b47f-bc7b711b8324"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dfd3ac59-843e-571d-b47f-bc7b711b8324" :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd3ac59-843e-571d-b47f-bc7b711b8324" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-21e9484e-2a02-53c8-af78-26814c64e2b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21e9484e-2a02-53c8-af78-26814c64e2b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21e9484e-2a02-53c8-af78-26814c64e2b3" :answer/id #uuid "21e9484e-2a02-53c8-af78-26814c64e2b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\ln 2+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-59250b6b-ada3-52c9-822b-a1bb62bb16a5" :activity/id #uuid "59250b6b-ada3-52c9-822b-a1bb62bb16a5" :activity/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Repeated Factors" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049227 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-fd6a53a2-cedf-57ed-83ba-72d0485606e6" "step-a1dbcc12-54b2-55ac-bd69-f6c875fd3cf4" "step-67e1f938-d404-58a9-83d5-e63e0a59f2a5" "step-069d670c-cd83-5d0b-9f6b-dc9fcde46e21" "step-288cf9b3-dd3f-5505-b4fa-8e8060a38d1d"] :activity/first-step "step-fd6a53a2-cedf-57ed-83ba-72d0485606e6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-fd6a53a2-cedf-57ed-83ba-72d0485606e6" :step/id #uuid "fd6a53a2-cedf-57ed-83ba-72d0485606e6" :step/content 17592186250438 :step/next "step-a1dbcc12-54b2-55ac-bd69-f6c875fd3cf4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a1dbcc12-54b2-55ac-bd69-f6c875fd3cf4" :step/id #uuid "a1dbcc12-54b2-55ac-bd69-f6c875fd3cf4" :step/content "question-6d6e949e-2c23-5f49-968a-1d0b9baadd85" :step/next "step-67e1f938-d404-58a9-83d5-e63e0a59f2a5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-67e1f938-d404-58a9-83d5-e63e0a59f2a5" :step/id #uuid "67e1f938-d404-58a9-83d5-e63e0a59f2a5" :step/content "question-4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45" :step/next "step-069d670c-cd83-5d0b-9f6b-dc9fcde46e21" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-069d670c-cd83-5d0b-9f6b-dc9fcde46e21" :step/id #uuid "069d670c-cd83-5d0b-9f6b-dc9fcde46e21" :step/content "question-d58d6a00-0092-54a9-834c-43383db09b35" :step/next "step-288cf9b3-dd3f-5505-b4fa-8e8060a38d1d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-288cf9b3-dd3f-5505-b4fa-8e8060a38d1d" :step/id #uuid "288cf9b3-dd3f-5505-b4fa-8e8060a38d1d" :step/content "question-fd717c83-b2bc-5cde-b553-20c9ad00a74f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231453 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a02f9513-4eff-5fed-8b27-1eb073c1f7dc" "question-8d772d0d-c3c0-582b-9807-e2b516a97226" "question-3ad87e41-53b3-5b37-8281-b66e69b1893c" "question-5ce73dda-6fa0-5e6a-8244-c7f7fa67bacd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231379 :question/problem "| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $3$ |\n| $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of $f$ on the set $S=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.$ Use the table to compute\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values in the table are $3,0,5,-1,$ and $2.$\n\nThe largest output is $5,$ and it occurs at $x=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThe smallest output is $-1,$ and it occurs at $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-1\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231454 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d22a2e95-90f5-5fc1-a867-e61a97b4c38b" "question-79c3ad8c-6f33-5a09-8a92-570cf7bc908c" "question-dc43d95b-1383-587c-8ba1-6694a0612d5c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231380 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb6079ac70816cd56061b8b353cc734176eed1698c50376a2e7a34c40f582a89.png)\n\nLet $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.$ The graph above shows the value of $f(x)$ for each $x \\in S.$ Use the graph to compute\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, the highest point has $y$-coordinate $5,$ and it occurs at $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{5}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}.\n$$\n\nThe lowest point has $y$-coordinate $0,$ and it occurs at $x=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a02f9513-4eff-5fed-8b27-1eb073c1f7dc" :question/id #uuid "a02f9513-4eff-5fed-8b27-1eb073c1f7dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-355248" :question/problem "| $t$ | $h(t)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $-5$ |\n| $0$ | $8$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |\n| $7$ | $-3$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of a function $h$ on the set $T=\\{-4,0,1,4,7\\}.$ Compute the following:\n\n${max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $-5,8,2,5,$ and $-3.$ The maximum output is $8$ (at $t=0$), and the minimum output is $-5$ (at $t=-4$). \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =0 \\\\ {min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1fdf6472-8e95-570d-bfbc-b65754f7fca4" "answer-field-de64455c-2dd7-5b45-8698-b87b3d241776" "answer-field-6771a7a7-a348-5d16-8f1e-5770d554ff43" "answer-field-a9d8067c-0c37-548f-94b0-8c25c2bb60a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1fdf6472-8e95-570d-bfbc-b65754f7fca4" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fdf6472-8e95-570d-bfbc-b65754f7fca4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7bb778f6-c4fa-5137-abdb-0ad5c14dacbc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bb778f6-c4fa-5137-abdb-0ad5c14dacbc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bb778f6-c4fa-5137-abdb-0ad5c14dacbc" :answer/id #uuid "7bb778f6-c4fa-5137-abdb-0a