images/28/2856c3b26abc3df90c228f4df3b95d9ee04a5c6cfd83cee78d1f6601c4bf8bdd.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cbeb2c34-b611-5622-babc-25dc1311116c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbeb2c34-b611-5622-babc-25dc1311116c" :question/id #uuid "cbeb2c34-b611-5622-babc-25dc1311116c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334309" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-3<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf45c67d-750d-505d-989a-2337e3e5243c" "answer-field-3ecaf0db-258e-5d47-8f5d-62e1aab6739b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf45c67d-750d-505d-989a-2337e3e5243c" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf45c67d-750d-505d-989a-2337e3e5243c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-620c9ad3-6c3f-5474-acac-ef780aed0dac" "answer-d292b233-658f-5ac4-a192-6e5e28d2210a" "answer-4cf21272-b21f-5d98-9834-5ad7916a9c8e" "answer-227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" "answer-ccd13b7f-f0ef-539c-8da4-07661c578421" "answer-18d50709-aa76-582b-9410-59b11f93d7c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-620c9ad3-6c3f-5474-acac-ef780aed0dac" :answer/id #uuid "620c9ad3-6c3f-5474-acac-ef780aed0dac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d292b233-658f-5ac4-a192-6e5e28d2210a" :answer/id #uuid "d292b233-658f-5ac4-a192-6e5e28d2210a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cf21272-b21f-5d98-9834-5ad7916a9c8e" :answer/id #uuid "4cf21272-b21f-5d98-9834-5ad7916a9c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer/id #uuid "227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccd13b7f-f0ef-539c-8da4-07661c578421" :answer/id #uuid "ccd13b7f-f0ef-539c-8da4-07661c578421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18d50709-aa76-582b-9410-59b11f93d7c9" :answer/id #uuid "18d50709-aa76-582b-9410-59b11f93d7c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ecaf0db-258e-5d47-8f5d-62e1aab6739b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecaf0db-258e-5d47-8f5d-62e1aab6739b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cb8140c-7d21-5826-b8c6-8681a1f60f7f" "answer-cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" "answer-c11c5670-2897-5382-a387-a92972ba5b80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cb8140c-7d21-5826-b8c6-8681a1f60f7f" :answer/id #uuid "3cb8140c-7d21-5826-b8c6-8681a1f60f7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a center" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer/id #uuid "cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c11c5670-2897-5382-a387-a92972ba5b80" :answer/id #uuid "c11c5670-2897-5382-a387-a92972ba5b80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/d1/d13abb97b171049a17544fcb312b71d2da3d9294788c1aebe7aa969e99b7819c.svg)\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054688 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c5f9c923-65fd-50b8-8fc2-266d6f6aaf68"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5f9c923-65fd-50b8-8fc2-266d6f6aaf68" :question/id #uuid "c5f9c923-65fd-50b8-8fc2-266d6f6aaf68" :question/math-academy-id "q-359057" :question/problem "Find the greatest common factor of the terms $25z$ and $10z.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $25$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,25.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,5,\\,10.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $25$ and $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,5.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $5.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $25z$ and $10z$ contain $z.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $25z$ and $10z$ is $5z.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39625f50-3002-571f-898f-be2df7b96b67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39625f50-3002-571f-898f-be2df7b96b67" :answer-field/id #uuid "39625f50-3002-571f-898f-be2df7b96b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d096e1f-d403-5074-b33d-861d3208db29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae3ddc69-bfec-56c2-ab78-f46921b4a9bc" "answer-3f424f7c-b371-573b-b654-2484dcfec880" "answer-68029331-733e-5701-95a2-1ae8b399e78e" "answer-0d096e1f-d403-5074-b33d-861d3208db29" "answer-93e76787-2539-53ca-946b-f238f8a4eaa2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3ddc69-bfec-56c2-ab78-f46921b4a9bc" :answer/id #uuid "ae3ddc69-bfec-56c2-ab78-f46921b4a9bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f424f7c-b371-573b-b654-2484dcfec880" :answer/id #uuid "3f424f7c-b371-573b-b654-2484dcfec880" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68029331-733e-5701-95a2-1ae8b399e78e" :answer/id #uuid "68029331-733e-5701-95a2-1ae8b399e78e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d096e1f-d403-5074-b33d-861d3208db29" :answer/id #uuid "0d096e1f-d403-5074-b33d-861d3208db29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93e76787-2539-53ca-946b-f238f8a4eaa2" :answer/id #uuid "93e76787-2539-53ca-946b-f238f8a4eaa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5e32c9fa-0237-548e-8a15-6b3954405e5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e32c9fa-0237-548e-8a15-6b3954405e5d" :question/id #uuid "5e32c9fa-0237-548e-8a15-6b3954405e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-326521" :question/problem "There were approximately $28,\\,000$ factory jobs in the city in $2020.$  Economic forecasts predict that this number will grow by $35%$ by the year $2030.$  Which of the following is closest to the projected number of factory jobs in $2030?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of jobs in $2030,$ we apply a $35%$ increase to the $2020$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $35%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+35%=135%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.35:$\n\n$$\n28,\\,000\\times 1.35=37,\\,800\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of factory jobs in $2030$ is $37,\\,800.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44d37873-44a7-53bb-92db-059de13aaea6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44d37873-44a7-53bb-92db-059de13aaea6" :answer-field/id #uuid "44d37873-44a7-53bb-92db-059de13aaea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ae391f5f-e535-5747-b4b8-4b5ea2b48a2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f30845c8-394b-56c3-81cf-79bedea33af1" "answer-def4830e-58b5-51ac-bf9e-18e4e7963563" "answer-ae391f5f-e535-5747-b4b8-4b5ea2b48a2e" "answer-52d40fbd-2937-5786-bc51-a9470c22a888" "answer-93e57907-9f82-5abe-8706-9554411efdb2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f30845c8-394b-56c3-81cf-79bedea33af1" :answer/id #uuid "f30845c8-394b-56c3-81cf-79bedea33af1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36,\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-def4830e-58b5-51ac-bf9e-18e4e7963563" :answer/id #uuid "def4830e-58b5-51ac-bf9e-18e4e7963563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae391f5f-e535-5747-b4b8-4b5ea2b48a2e" :answer/id #uuid "ae391f5f-e535-5747-b4b8-4b5ea2b48a2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37,\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52d40fbd-2937-5786-bc51-a9470c22a888" :answer/id #uuid "52d40fbd-2937-5786-bc51-a9470c22a888" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "38,\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93e57907-9f82-5abe-8706-9554411efdb2" :answer/id #uuid "93e57907-9f82-5abe-8706-9554411efdb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-320d3413-9845-54f4-9740-6e67695ad26e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-320d3413-9845-54f4-9740-6e67695ad26e" :question/id #uuid "320d3413-9845-54f4-9740-6e67695ad26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111226" :question/problem "Classify the polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ according to its degree and the number of terms." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $1,$ so it is a linear.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a linear monomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f60f859d-bc35-5505-9e52-c51c8f4b4755"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f60f859d-bc35-5505-9e52-c51c8f4b4755" :answer-field/id #uuid "f60f859d-bc35-5505-9e52-c51c8f4b4755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-28fabcc4-4d05-51ac-a912-143729df6bdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b8222ef-3b82-5715-9c8a-28e90a679db9" "answer-1be57fd3-5b10-52bd-9852-c6f7411b7f55" "answer-28fabcc4-4d05-51ac-a912-143729df6bdf" "answer-00d2ca87-06b5-5f73-a7a2-0d924de094ee" "answer-8b0e746a-0ea4-5fe0-96a8-19184993385d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b8222ef-3b82-5715-9c8a-28e90a679db9" :answer/id #uuid "3b8222ef-3b82-5715-9c8a-28e90a679db9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cubic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1be57fd3-5b10-52bd-9852-c6f7411b7f55" :answer/id #uuid "1be57fd3-5b10-52bd-9852-c6f7411b7f55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "linear binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28fabcc4-4d05-51ac-a912-143729df6bdf" :answer/id #uuid "28fabcc4-4d05-51ac-a912-143729df6bdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "linear monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00d2ca87-06b5-5f73-a7a2-0d924de094ee" :answer/id #uuid "00d2ca87-06b5-5f73-a7a2-0d924de094ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quadratic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0e746a-0ea4-5fe0-96a8-19184993385d" :answer/id #uuid "8b0e746a-0ea4-5fe0-96a8-19184993385d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quartic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073885 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bad718eb-7fd4-52e5-a648-56b0a89dfa07"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bad718eb-7fd4-52e5-a648-56b0a89dfa07" :question/id #uuid "bad718eb-7fd4-52e5-a648-56b0a89dfa07" :question/math-academy-id "q-93111" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c4f090ff980be893416857fde0665bce955cee19f6a9dd7634039cbbcc641b6.png)\n\nAlex is making concrete using crushed stone and cement. The amount of cement he needs for a given amount of crushed stone is shown above. What is the ratio of crushed stone to cement in its simplest form?" :question/worked-solution "One of the points belonging to the graph is $(60,15).$ Let's write the ratio $60:15$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $15:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{60÷15}{15÷15}=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio of crushed stone to cement is $4:1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ffde6de-d8c6-5fc9-81ca-8ebe53a5d344"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ffde6de-d8c6-5fc9-81ca-8ebe53a5d344" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ffde6de-d8c6-5fc9-81ca-8ebe53a5d344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-40f230c7-cc08-501f-871e-4e5e7ab14dfc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eec5ef68-1664-57b4-92d4-0ce802cf7911" "answer-740f6103-8614-52d1-9118-629ed12e15e5" "answer-5e6fc0dd-5148-5e39-a2ad-eec06de00b38" "answer-40f230c7-cc08-501f-871e-4e5e7ab14dfc" "answer-dbe3e1e4-034c-5deb-928b-54a12e7789ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eec5ef68-1664-57b4-92d4-0ce802cf7911" :answer/id #uuid "eec5ef68-1664-57b4-92d4-0ce802cf7911" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-740f6103-8614-52d1-9118-629ed12e15e5" :answer/id #uuid "740f6103-8614-52d1-9118-629ed12e15e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e6fc0dd-5148-5e39-a2ad-eec06de00b38" :answer/id #uuid "5e6fc0dd-5148-5e39-a2ad-eec06de00b38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3:1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40f230c7-cc08-501f-871e-4e5e7ab14dfc" :answer/id #uuid "40f230c7-cc08-501f-871e-4e5e7ab14dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4:1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbe3e1e4-034c-5deb-928b-54a12e7789ae" :answer/id #uuid "dbe3e1e4-034c-5deb-928b-54a12e7789ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4:3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073820 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c77dd09-2d15-50f1-838b-9b9dd6ed6be5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c77dd09-2d15-50f1-838b-9b9dd6ed6be5" :question/id #uuid "2c77dd09-2d15-50f1-838b-9b9dd6ed6be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-178541" :question/problem "The additive inverse of $30$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the additive inverse of the number $30$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,(30)=-30.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n30+(-30)=30-30=0\\,✓\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c6617d4-5306-575e-8610-98ba6a3acfd0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c6617d4-5306-575e-8610-98ba6a3acfd0" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6617d4-5306-575e-8610-98ba6a3acfd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1f33fa7f-86a4-5672-82f4-90c12943702f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f33fa7f-86a4-5672-82f4-90c12943702f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f33fa7f-86a4-5672-82f4-90c12943702f" :answer/id #uuid "1f33fa7f-86a4-5672-82f4-90c12943702f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119960 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15f99678-56db-56d3-a971-804057202f02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-15f99678-56db-56d3-a971-804057202f02" :question/id #uuid "15f99678-56db-56d3-a971-804057202f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-186161" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{-1,000}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the number $-1,000$ is a perfect cube.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-1,000},$ we express $-1,000$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1,000} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)\\cdot (-10)\\cdot (-10)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = \\\\ -10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6d9b3c0-3abe-5e9b-971f-c0fb9407dba7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6d9b3c0-3abe-5e9b-971f-c0fb9407dba7" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d9b3c0-3abe-5e9b-971f-c0fb9407dba7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6e088514-199a-567e-82fa-8f6660eeb0c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e088514-199a-567e-82fa-8f6660eeb0c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e088514-199a-567e-82fa-8f6660eeb0c8" :answer/id #uuid "6e088514-199a-567e-82fa-8f6660eeb0c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138537 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4c36616-a95f-51f2-89f5-60b31bb5cb88"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4c36616-a95f-51f2-89f5-60b31bb5cb88" :question/id #uuid "e4c36616-a95f-51f2-89f5-60b31bb5cb88" :question/math-academy-id "q-181316" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b96cbe8ea09940b84c1d9d03fc55bdb82c61388bfb66d99cc7c6abd847a1fd.png)\n\nThe inequality corresponding to the highlighted region shown above is $x${{field-1}}\n$-3\\text{ or }x$\n{{field-2}}$1.$" :question/worked-solution "The red rays contain all values less than $-3,$ or greater than $1.$ In addition, \n\n- the closed circle at $-3$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-3,$ and\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x\\le -3$ or $x>1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ef4eccd-b77f-5147-9424-093fafb1f473" "answer-field-846433e8-6e1d-515f-aa50-49417beb1847"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ef4eccd-b77f-5147-9424-093fafb1f473" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef4eccd-b77f-5147-9424-093fafb1f473" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e6772599-5098-5df2-a83a-02ac5400c6dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6772599-5098-5df2-a83a-02ac5400c6dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6772599-5098-5df2-a83a-02ac5400c6dd" :answer/id #uuid "e6772599-5098-5df2-a83a-02ac5400c6dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-846433e8-6e1d-515f-aa50-49417beb1847" :answer-field/id #uuid "846433e8-6e1d-515f-aa50-49417beb1847" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f884cbed-e942-5458-b509-5ef52cf124ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f884cbed-e942-5458-b509-5ef52cf124ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f884cbed-e942-5458-b509-5ef52cf124ff" :answer/id #uuid "f884cbed-e942-5458-b509-5ef52cf124ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value ">" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35308452-763c-5cea-932c-69c3b89da098"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-35308452-763c-5cea-932c-69c3b89da098" :question/id #uuid "35308452-763c-5cea-932c-69c3b89da098" :question/math-academy-id "q-187260" :question/problem "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $2$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $18$ |" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $4:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($6$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $2,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $18,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\times 6}{3\\times 6} & =\\frac{12}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $18$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a436e5e-a9bf-5f9f-964b-11b2e975c97d" "answer-field-15e10b26-4030-53ed-ab51-362142804b18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a436e5e-a9bf-5f9f-964b-11b2e975c97d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a436e5e-a9bf-5f9f-964b-11b2e975c97d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d44df9e2-b2da-59ea-baa5-21bdd925757e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d44df9e2-b2da-59ea-baa5-21bdd925757e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d44df9e2-b2da-59ea-baa5-21bdd925757e" :answer/id #uuid "d44df9e2-b2da-59ea-baa5-21bdd925757e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15e10b26-4030-53ed-ab51-362142804b18" :answer-field/id #uuid "15e10b26-4030-53ed-ab51-362142804b18" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-01f11c58-0e5c-5a4e-97bf-ac4e376a8081" :answer-field/choices ["answer-01f11c58-0e5c-5a4e-97bf-ac4e376a8081"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01f11c58-0e5c-5a4e-97bf-ac4e376a8081" :answer/id #uuid "01f11c58-0e5c-5a4e-97bf-ac4e376a8081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" "question-6581b3e4-1dde-5fc5-a59c-9d058401fe58"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" :question/id #uuid "14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-555460" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{30}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{30}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $30$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{30}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36$.\nSince $25<30<36$, we know that $5<\\sqrt{30}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.3}^{2} & =28.09 \\\\ {5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.5}^{2} & =30.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $29.16<30<30.25$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.4}^{2}<30<{5.5}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.4<\\sqrt{30}<5.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{30}$ lies between $5.4$ and $5.5$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6a4769f-21c1-52e4-82b5-a8a4ff749939"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6a4769f-21c1-52e4-82b5-a8a4ff749939" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a4769f-21c1-52e4-82b5-a8a4ff749939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-141002c6-b340-5ab9-884d-d4f6694527f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21b63af1-c719-5e54-b9fb-f8ac242a95b2" "answer-d7c71876-374e-5a61-9b3a-791e08c878c9" "answer-141002c6-b340-5ab9-884d-d4f6694527f2" "answer-073f9210-d9a2-5f36-800b-c48c70348e31" "answer-a2884d9c-c9cc-5d25-8ed3-7e9a3f85ac60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21b63af1-c719-5e54-b9fb-f8ac242a95b2" :answer/id #uuid "21b63af1-c719-5e54-b9fb-f8ac242a95b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.2$ and $5.3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7c71876-374e-5a61-9b3a-791e08c878c9" :answer/id #uuid "d7c71876-374e-5a61-9b3a-791e08c878c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.3$ and $5.4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-141002c6-b340-5ab9-884d-d4f6694527f2" :answer/id #uuid "141002c6-b340-5ab9-884d-d4f6694527f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.4$ and $5.5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-073f9210-d9a2-5f36-800b-c48c70348e31" :answer/id #uuid "073f9210-d9a2-5f36-800b-c48c70348e31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.5$ and $5.6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2884d9c-c9cc-5d25-8ed3-7e9a3f85ac60" :answer/id #uuid "a2884d9c-c9cc-5d25-8ed3-7e9a3f85ac60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.6$ and $5.7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" "question-5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" :question/id #uuid "3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-555923" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{72},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{72}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{72},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $72.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<72<81,$ we know that the square root of $72$ lies between $8$ and $9.$\n\n![](images/e9/e931f5e15b35adb850966e40e4ad759c233d9419771998efba5dc91d9ab95bc1.png)\n\nSince $72$ lies between $64$ and $81,$ we expect $\\sqrt{72}$ to lie between $8$ and $9.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.4}^{2} & =70.56 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $72$ lies between $70.56$ and $72.25,$ so $8.4<\\sqrt{72}<8.5.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $72:$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.4}^{2}$ is $72-70.56=1.44.$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.5}^{2}$ is $72.25-72=0.25.$\n\nSince $72$ is much closer to $72.25,$ $\\sqrt{72}$ is closer to $8.5.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{72}≈8.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5cdc719-89df-501a-9054-6f1aff3d4e0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5cdc719-89df-501a-9054-6f1aff3d4e0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5cdc719-89df-501a-9054-6f1aff3d4e0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cee2d1b8-abc0-540d-91d0-4123cd119263" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cee2d1b8-abc0-540d-91d0-4123cd119263"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cee2d1b8-abc0-540d-91d0-4123cd119263" :answer/id #uuid "cee2d1b8-abc0-540d-91d0-4123cd119263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ec99099b-9970-5301-8219-551157f9c01b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec99099b-9970-5301-8219-551157f9c01b" :question/id #uuid "ec99099b-9970-5301-8219-551157f9c01b" :question/math-academy-id "q-556441" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt{45}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{2}=36$ and ${7}^{2}=49.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{2}=36$ and ${7}^{2}=49.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between $3$ and $4,$ since ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{120}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{30}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt{45}$ or $\\sqrt{40}.$\n\nSince $45>40,$ we know that $\\sqrt{45}>\\sqrt{40}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{45}$ is the greatest number among the given options." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7df3e984-bbc8-5964-9312-1a2d645cc83b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7df3e984-bbc8-5964-9312-1a2d645cc83b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7df3e984-bbc8-5964-9312-1a2d645cc83b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b64804d5-9f57-59e0-925d-cefea393e51d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7a88941-27e7-5202-b0d9-8572edf2d3c5" "answer-3c8ec4b2-8a62-5be2-bc85-cb1f42b10068" "answer-b335f7ec-1759-5795-9639-e0da48f10014" "answer-47a27d49-a40e-50b5-a1b0-9a2ba16e708a" "answer-b64804d5-9f57-59e0-925d-cefea393e51d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7a88941-27e7-5202-b0d9-8572edf2d3c5" :answer/id #uuid "e7a88941-27e7-5202-b0d9-8572edf2d3c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{120}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c8ec4b2-8a62-5be2-bc85-cb1f42b10068" :answer/id #uuid "3c8ec4b2-8a62-5be2-bc85-cb1f42b10068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b335f7ec-1759-5795-9639-e0da48f10014" :answer/id #uuid "b335f7ec-1759-5795-9639-e0da48f10014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47a27d49-a40e-50b5-a1b0-9a2ba16e708a" :answer/id #uuid "47a27d49-a40e-50b5-a1b0-9a2ba16e708a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b64804d5-9f57-59e0-925d-cefea393e51d" :answer/id #uuid "b64804d5-9f57-59e0-925d-cefea393e51d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6581b3e4-1dde-5fc5-a59c-9d058401fe58" :question/id #uuid "6581b3e4-1dde-5fc5-a59c-9d058401fe58" :question/math-academy-id "q-556451" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt{88}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the two consecutive whole numbers that bracket $\\sqrt{88}.$ We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above $88:$\n\n$$\n81<88<100\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{81}<\\sqrt{88}<\\sqrt{100},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n9<\\sqrt{88}<10.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that $\\sqrt{88}$ is between $9$ and $10.$ \n\nTo narrow this down to two consecutive tenths, we test the squares of decimals between $9.0$ and $10.0.$ Since $88$ is closer to $81$ than to $100,$ the value of $\\sqrt{88}$ will be closer to $9$ than to $10.$ We can start testing at $9.3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.3}^{2} & =86.49 \\\\ {9.4}^{2} & =88.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $88$ falls between $86.49$ and $88.36:$\n\n$$\n86.49<88<88.36\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{86.49}<\\sqrt{88}<\\sqrt{88.36},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n9.3<\\sqrt{88}<9.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{88}$ lies between the consecutive tenths $a=9.3$ and $b=9.4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d99708c6-cfde-5abe-b62f-e0c5ade1ed06" "answer-field-106ad80c-f5c2-5906-acd9-99f57be5d5c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d99708c6-cfde-5abe-b62f-e0c5ade1ed06" :answer-field/id #uuid "d99708c6-cfde-5abe-b62f-e0c5ade1ed06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4141bac0-74e9-56ec-bc66-46fada0ad9dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4141bac0-74e9-56ec-bc66-46fada0ad9dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4141bac0-74e9-56ec-bc66-46fada0ad9dc" :answer/id #uuid "4141bac0-74e9-56ec-bc66-46fada0ad9dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-106ad80c-f5c2-5906-acd9-99f57be5d5c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "106ad80c-f5c2-5906-acd9-99f57be5d5c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0d93e06b-de31-5b20-b728-e05b071741e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d93e06b-de31-5b20-b728-e05b071741e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d93e06b-de31-5b20-b728-e05b071741e6" :answer/id #uuid "0d93e06b-de31-5b20-b728-e05b071741e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b" :question/id #uuid "5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b" :question/math-academy-id "q-556465" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{28},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{28}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{28},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $28.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<28<36,$ we know that the square root of $28$ lies between $5$ and $6.$\n\n![](images/9f/9f16093fe61b05142060e1dcc36cf824ec664b271499d24758831a6bc53757ac.png)\n\nSince $28$ lies between $25$ and $36,$ we expect $\\sqrt{28}$ to lie between $5$ and $6.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{2} & =27.04 \\\\ {5.3}^{2} & =28.09\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $28$ lies between $27.04$ and $28.09,$ so $5.2<\\sqrt{28}<5.3.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $28:$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.2}^{2}$ is $28-27.04=0.96.$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.3}^{2}$ is $28.09-28=0.09.$\n\nSince $28$ is much closer to $28.09,$ $\\sqrt{28}$ is closer to $5.3.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{28}≈5.3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ba38eb1-b536-562c-bede-07820f4ec156"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ba38eb1-b536-562c-bede-07820f4ec156" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba38eb1-b536-562c-bede-07820f4ec156" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-addca03f-013a-5203-a880-de3131df5bc2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-addca03f-013a-5203-a880-de3131df5bc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-addca03f-013a-5203-a880-de3131df5bc2" :answer/id #uuid "addca03f-013a-5203-a880-de3131df5bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2f0ca7fd-efb9-5658-bd3a-533b2ddcfe17" :activity/id #uuid "2f0ca7fd-efb9-5658-bd3a-533b2ddcfe17" :activity/title "Approximating Square Roots and Cube Roots" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186499633 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-0cffe8b2-9eaa-5609-8875-f9176136dbf8" "step-463a2187-c3fe-599b-aa5d-f1c756b0cc9c" "step-ad31145b-e901-58de-9d30-fd62353a75b5" "step-1d3ce030-a464-5cad-80c6-c2925c812161" "step-1186a56e-84b6-50a6-9e81-6065bd0a7d6e"] :activity/first-step "step-0cffe8b2-9eaa-5609-8875-f9176136dbf8" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0cffe8b2-9eaa-5609-8875-f9176136dbf8" :step/id #uuid "0cffe8b2-9eaa-5609-8875-f9176136dbf8" :step/content "question-14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" :step/next "step-463a2187-c3fe-599b-aa5d-f1c756b0cc9c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-463a2187-c3fe-599b-aa5d-f1c756b0cc9c" :step/id #uuid "463a2187-c3fe-599b-aa5d-f1c756b0cc9c" :step/content "question-3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" :step/next "step-ad31145b-e901-58de-9d30-fd62353a75b5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad31145b-e901-58de-9d30-fd62353a75b5" :step/id #uuid "ad31145b-e901-58de-9d30-fd62353a75b5" :step/content "question-ec99099b-9970-5301-8219-551157f9c01b" :step/next "step-1d3ce030-a464-5cad-80c6-c2925c812161" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1d3ce030-a464-5cad-80c6-c2925c812161" :step/id #uuid "1d3ce030-a464-5cad-80c6-c2925c812161" :step/content "question-6581b3e4-1dde-5fc5-a59c-9d058401fe58" :step/next "step-1186a56e-84b6-50a6-9e81-6065bd0a7d6e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1186a56e-84b6-50a6-9e81-6065bd0a7d6e" :step/id #uuid "1186a56e-84b6-50a6-9e81-6065bd0a7d6e" :step/content "question-5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120249 :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{-8}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the number $-8$ is a perfect cube.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-8},$ we express $-8$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-2)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = \\\\ -2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117653 :answer-field/correct 17592186117655 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119959 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7fc0fee8-5d8f-576c-8b1d-3594d43c9abb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fc0fee8-5d8f-576c-8b1d-3594d43c9abb" :question/id #uuid "7fc0fee8-5d8f-576c-8b1d-3594d43c9abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-44083" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{64}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $64$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{64}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{64}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{64}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-406ab344-f9fd-5033-9ed9-b2f83ded5ae0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-406ab344-f9fd-5033-9ed9-b2f83ded5ae0" :answer-field/id #uuid "406ab344-f9fd-5033-9ed9-b2f83ded5ae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4fd76159-2140-5346-98a1-4afe816c2e3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb0bdda5-9a04-5aae-a0c0-3728d75cd1ec" "answer-e8be0511-cfcd-558e-ab87-0f2fcf576a90" "answer-a62ca124-9da6-5b38-bae4-21401dc99670" "answer-df606574-cd00-549e-b533-e130e89eaadb" "answer-4fd76159-2140-5346-98a1-4afe816c2e3d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb0bdda5-9a04-5aae-a0c0-3728d75cd1ec" :answer/id #uuid "eb0bdda5-9a04-5aae-a0c0-3728d75cd1ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8be0511-cfcd-558e-ab87-0f2fcf576a90" :answer/id #uuid "e8be0511-cfcd-558e-ab87-0f2fcf576a90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a62ca124-9da6-5b38-bae4-21401dc99670" :answer/id #uuid "a62ca124-9da6-5b38-bae4-21401dc99670" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df606574-cd00-549e-b533-e130e89eaadb" :answer/id #uuid "df606574-cd00-549e-b533-e130e89eaadb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fd76159-2140-5346-98a1-4afe816c2e3d" :answer/id #uuid "4fd76159-2140-5346-98a1-4afe816c2e3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6550081-06ce-5fdd-8f86-5cb7f9262731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6550081-06ce-5fdd-8f86-5cb7f9262731" :question/id #uuid "a6550081-06ce-5fdd-8f86-5cb7f9262731" :question/math-academy-id "q-71502" :question/problem "$(\\sqrt[3]{-343})^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{-343})^{3}=-343.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69802b94-6643-58fc-ad81-08af38307602"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69802b94-6643-58fc-ad81-08af38307602" :answer-field/id #uuid "69802b94-6643-58fc-ad81-08af38307602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b467886-d4bd-53f1-975f-d7a82a45d740" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b467886-d4bd-53f1-975f-d7a82a45d740" "answer-8346c2e4-3159-519a-933c-ed33451c4b6a" "answer-3e32be5a-ab1f-58fc-8cd8-39d0fdebf2af" "answer-39a10731-5fc5-5806-9d35-87c5df736739" "answer-6b798186-aa8c-5b3b-91a6-72f60d4af904"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b467886-d4bd-53f1-975f-d7a82a45d740" :answer/id #uuid "9b467886-d4bd-53f1-975f-d7a82a45d740" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-343" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8346c2e4-3159-519a-933c-ed33451c4b6a" :answer/id #uuid "8346c2e4-3159-519a-933c-ed33451c4b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e32be5a-ab1f-58fc-8cd8-39d0fdebf2af" :answer/id #uuid "3e32be5a-ab1f-58fc-8cd8-39d0fdebf2af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "343" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39a10731-5fc5-5806-9d35-87c5df736739" :answer/id #uuid "39a10731-5fc5-5806-9d35-87c5df736739" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b798186-aa8c-5b3b-91a6-72f60d4af904" :answer/id #uuid "6b798186-aa8c-5b3b-91a6-72f60d4af904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6c37c474-6faf-5286-9e5b-310294f87a2f" :activity/id #uuid "6c37c474-6faf-5286-9e5b-310294f87a2f" :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048770 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-1e5d4b1f-71f8-513d-8ca3-3ea352241efb" "step-646a95eb-3d50-5de4-b371-82aed596236d" "step-e9d03a39-8b21-5275-aa96-f0cc96d800f3"] :activity/first-step "step-1e5d4b1f-71f8-513d-8ca3-3ea352241efb" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e5d4b1f-71f8-513d-8ca3-3ea352241efb" :step/id #uuid "1e5d4b1f-71f8-513d-8ca3-3ea352241efb" :step/content 17592186120249 :step/next "step-646a95eb-3d50-5de4-b371-82aed596236d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-646a95eb-3d50-5de4-b371-82aed596236d" :step/id #uuid "646a95eb-3d50-5de4-b371-82aed596236d" :step/content "question-7fc0fee8-5d8f-576c-8b1d-3594d43c9abb" :step/next "step-e9d03a39-8b21-5275-aa96-f0cc96d800f3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9d03a39-8b21-5275-aa96-f0cc96d800f3" :step/id #uuid "e9d03a39-8b21-5275-aa96-f0cc96d800f3" :step/content "question-a6550081-06ce-5fdd-8f86-5cb7f9262731" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055233 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186052635 :answer-field/correct 17592186052640 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233995 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db0ef0b5-fcba-553e-ac8c-0225ffb723a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db0ef0b5-fcba-553e-ac8c-0225ffb723a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "db0ef0b5-fcba-553e-ac8c-0225ffb723a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6cc339c6-f7b7-5c24-ab62-8e1ff9e4ed3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6cc339c6-f7b7-5c24-ab62-8e1ff9e4ed3a" "answer-f40b5418-f57a-574c-a2f2-815b0c4b394d" "answer-e6d0edc5-1ae9-515d-a0fc-bd1888788abb" "answer-5797e9e9-6e67-537d-a655-4